{"parentUuid":"ea8067dc-00cd-4cd4-8469-e9a8f631716f","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011Ch7kWynFg6EYFShbakttH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":961,"cache_read_input_tokens":136131,"output_tokens":4208,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":19},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":961,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":4208,"cache_read_input_tokens":136131,"cache_creation_input_tokens":961,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":961},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":637,"thinkingDisplay":"updates","serverClassifierRequest":"056e8e1b-0024-4bea-b754-1c28ba40da0e","apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011Ch7kWybM7KL32KxoNBH8P","requestRef":"3c69ac0a-f6ac-4c13-816e-dc2db6c4d8b3","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5","promptCacheRequested":"1h","type":"assistant","uuid":"d0041ecf-5941-4083-bdeb-fbb19227bd3b","timestamp":"2026-11-17T02:58:36.317Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"xhigh","perTurnEffort":null,"session_id":"1041b1a0-c015-41c2-a29f-4f2db2f6e0cb","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv","sessionId":"93ae8948-c612-4072-8e70-2750e80df9ce","version":"2.1.323","gitBranch":"main","slug":"floofy-discovering-bachman"}
{"parentUuid":"d0041ecf-5941-4083-bdeb-fbb19227bd3b","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011Ch7kWynFg6EYFShbakttH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01PUp2LCtWBPME2gKC8fXwuZ","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"set -e\nKIT=/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-suhaan-devenv/93ae8948-c612-4072-8e70-2750e80df9ce/scratchpad/winkit\ncat > \"$KIT/TESTING.md\" <<'EOF'\n# Windows verification protocol\n\nRun this on a **real Windows machine**. Not WSL, not a Linux container, not Wine.\nWSL is a Linux kernel and will pass checks that Windows fails; it is the exact\nfalse green this kit exists to avoid.\n\nBudget about **20 minutes**, most of it the full test suite in section 5.\n\n---\n\n## 0. Prerequisites\n\n| Need | Check | If missing |\n|---|---|---|\n| Windows 10 or 11, native | — | stop, this kit is useless elsewhere |\n| PowerShell 5.1 or `pwsh` 7+ | `$PSVersionTable.PSVersion` | ships with Windows |\n| Go 1.26+ | `go version` | https://go.dev/dl/ — stop if absent |\n| Git for Windows | `git --version` | needed: one command under test is repo-scoped |\n\n`go version` must report `windows/amd64` or `windows/arm64`. If it says `linux`,\nyou are in WSL — open a real PowerShell instead.\n\nOptional, and it changes what section 1 can prove: the **host `entire` CLI** on\nPATH (`entire --version`). With it, section 1 runs the strong check. Without it,\nsection 1 falls back to a weaker one and says so. Do not install it just for\nthis; just report which you got.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Get the code\n\n```powershell\ngit clone <this repo's URL> brain-win\ncd brain-win\n```\n\nThe default branch holds the fix. Confirm you have three commits and that the\nmiddle two are the change under test:\n\n```powershell\ngit log --oneline\n```\n\nExpect, newest first:\n\n```\n<sha>  docs(limits): record the Windows path-length ceiling and narrow a lock claim\n<sha>  fix(windows): make the brain agree with the host on Windows\n<sha>  baseline: entire-brain origin/main @ 8cab9ba6\n```\n\nThe `baseline` tag points at that third commit. It is an untouched export of\nupstream `main` and exists for exactly one purpose: section 6.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Run the script\n\n```powershell\npwsh -File windows-verify.ps1\n```\n\nor, on PowerShell 5.1:\n\n```powershell\npowershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File windows-verify.ps1\n```\n\nIt refuses to run outside the repo root, and refuses to run without `go` and\n`git`, rather than failing confusingly three sections in. It prints `PASS` /\n`FAIL` / `..` per check and a summary at the end.\n\n**Capture the entire output.** The simplest way:\n\n```powershell\npwsh -File windows-verify.ps1 2>&1 | Tee-Object -FilePath verify-output.txt\n```\n\n---\n\n## 3. What each section actually proves\n\nThis is the part that matters more than the PASS lines. Several checks are\nweaker than their name suggests, and one thing under test is not covered at all.\n\n### Section 1 — store path\n\n**The defect:** the brain resolved its data directory through XDG and\n`~/.local/share` on every OS, while the host CLI ignores XDG on Windows and uses\n`%LOCALAPPDATA%`. A dispatched run was fine, because the host sets\n`ENTIRE_PLUGIN_DATA_DIR` explicitly. A **standalone** `entire-brain.exe` read a\ndifferent, empty store and said nothing about it.\n\n- **Strong check**, only if the host `entire` CLI is present: standalone and\n  dispatched runs must resolve the *same* store path. If they differ, the fix\n  did not work — that is the bug verbatim.\n- **Weak check**, the fallback: the resolved path merely sits under\n  `%LOCALAPPDATA%`. That is necessary but not sufficient.\n\nThe script clears `ENTIRE_PLUGIN_*_DIR` first, deliberately: the fix only fires\nwhen `ENTIRE_PLUGIN_DATA_DIR` is unset, so a test that sets it tests nothing.\n\n### Section 2 — `ProcessAlive`\n\n**The defect:** `os.FindProcess` succeeds for a process that has already exited\nwhile a handle to it is still open — and `os.FindProcess` itself holds exactly\nsuch a handle. So a finished backfill reported as *running* forever, and the\nguard that prevents a second backfill would never let one start again.\n\nRuns `TestProcessAlive` in `internal/cli/internal/brainstore`. These tests are\nWindows-only; on any other OS they do not exist, which is why they have never\nrun. Green here is real signal.\n\n### Section 3 — doctor warning for synced/UNC stores\n\n**The defect:** `%USERPROFILE%` is commonly OneDrive-redirected, and SQLite WAL\non a synced directory corrupts. Brain uses WAL and said nothing.\n\n**This section is conditional and will usually prove the negative half only.**\nIf your profile is *not* OneDrive-redirected, it checks that doctor does **not**\nwarn — real, but it is the no-false-positive half. The warning path itself only\ngets exercised on a machine whose store genuinely lands under OneDrive or a UNC\nshare. If yours does, say so loudly in your report; that is the valuable case.\n\n### Section 4 — long paths\n\n**The measurement:** the export tree's path *below the store root* reaches 277\ncharacters, and classic Win32 caps a path at 260 including the root. On paper\nevery deep export path is over the limit. It works only because Go rewrites long\n**absolute** paths to the `\\\\?\\` extended form before calling Win32 — which\ncovers Go's own `os` package and nothing else.\n\nSo this section writes a 277-char-shaped path under `%TEMP%` and reports whether\nit succeeded, plus your machine's `LongPathsEnabled` registry value.\n\n- A **PASS with `LongPathsEnabled` absent or 0** is the interesting result: it\n  confirms the margin is zero and that a Go implementation detail is the only\n  thing holding it up.\n- A **FAIL** means deep export paths are a live user bug on this machine. Report\n  the character count and the exact error.\n\nDo **not** enable `LongPathsEnabled` to make this pass. You would be measuring a\ndifferent machine.\n\n### Section 5 — full test suite\n\n`go test ./...` across the module. This is roughly what the project's Windows CI\nleg runs. It is the regression net: it proves the changes did not break the\n~42 packages that already worked.\n\nIt also compiles `*_windows.go` files that nothing else here executes — notably\nthe atomic-replace retry (see the gap below).\n\n### Not covered by the script at all\n\n- **Detached backfill** — needs a human. Section 4 of this document below.\n- **Atomic-replace retry on sharing violations** (`internal/config/atomic_replace_windows.go`).\n  Nothing in this kit reproduces a `SHARING_VIOLATION`; it needs a second\n  process holding the file open at the moment of replace. The suite proves it\n  compiles and does not regress, not that the retry fires. **Honest gap. Do not\n  report it as verified.**\n- **The windows/arm64 CI leg** (`.github/workflows/test.yml`). Only a CI run can\n  prove that, and nothing here pushes. Unverifiable in this kit by design.\n\n---\n\n## 4. The manual check (please do this one)\n\nThe script cannot test it, and it is the most user-visible fix in the set.\n\n**The defect:** `CREATE_NEW_PROCESS_GROUP` alone does not detach a child from the\nconsole. Closing the terminal killed the background backfill — the single thing\nthe detached spawn exists to prevent.\n\n```powershell\n# in some git repository on this machine, NOT this kit\nentire brain setup\n```\n\nThen, **while the background backfill is still running**, close the terminal\nwindow — click the X, do not Ctrl-C and do not `exit`.\n\nCheck two things:\n\n1. **Open Task Manager** (or `Get-Process entire-brain`). `entire-brain.exe`\n   should **still be running**. Before the fix it died with the console.\n2. **No console window should have flashed** on screen when the backfill started.\n\nReport: did the process survive, and did a window flash? If you cannot run\n`entire brain setup` because the host CLI is not installed, say so — that is a\nlegitimate \"not run\", not a failure.\n\n> Deliberately **not** `CREATE_NO_WINDOW`: Microsoft documents that flag as\n> undefined in combination with `DETACHED_PROCESS`. If you see a brief console\n> flash, report it rather than assuming it is the bug — it may be the documented\n> tradeoff.\n\n---\n\n## 5. If the full suite fails\n\nDo not report a suite failure as a regression until you have checked whether it\nis pre-existing. The `baseline` tag is there for this.\n\n```powershell\n# note the failing package from the section 5 output, e.g. ./internal/cli\ngo test ./internal/cli -count=1          # on the fix: should reproduce\ngit checkout baseline\ngo test ./internal/cli -count=1          # on untouched upstream\ngit checkout -                           # back to the fix\n```\n\n- Fails on **both** → pre-existing upstream failure, **not** caused by this\n  change. Still worth reporting, labelled as pre-existing.\n- Fails only on the **fix** → a real regression. This is the highest-value\n  finding in the whole exercise. Report the full test output.\n\nRun the suite with `-count=1` to defeat Go's test cache, and if a failure looks\nlike it might be load- or timing-related, re-run that one package alone before\ncalling it.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Things that are **not** bugs\n\nDo not report these as failures.\n\n- **`..` lines in the output.** Those are informational, not failures. Only\n  `FAIL` counts, and the summary lists them.\n- **\"host 'entire' CLI not on PATH; skipped...\"** — expected if you did not\n  install it. Section 1 just ran its weaker half.\n- **\"profile is not OneDrive-redirected\"** — expected on most machines. Section 3\n  ran its negative half.\n- **Section 4 passing with `LongPathsEnabled` empty or 0.** That is the expected\n  and most informative outcome.\n- **Capture hooks doing nothing.** This kit ships `.entire/settings.json` with\n  `enabled: false` and has `.claude/settings.json` removed, on purpose: the real\n  file invokes hooks via `sh -c`, and in a throwaway clone it would attribute\n  checkpoints to the wrong repository. Both are kit-local changes and are **not**\n  part of the fix under test.\n\n### One thing worth noticing while you are there\n\nThe real repo's `.claude/settings.json` (removed here, visible in the `baseline`\ncommit) wires every hook as `sh -c '...'`. Git for Windows installs `sh.exe`\noutside the default PATH, so on a machine without Git Bash on PATH those hooks\nfail at every hook event. **If you have a moment**, check whether `sh` resolves\non your machine:\n\n```powershell\nGet-Command sh -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue\n```\n\nReport the answer. It is not part of this change, but it is a plausible Windows\ndefect in the project and nobody has checked it on real hardware.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Report back\n\nFill in `RESULTS-TEMPLATE.md` and send it back. The things that must be in it:\n\n1. Windows version, `go version` output, `$PSVersionTable.PSVersion`, and\n   whether the host `entire` CLI was present.\n2. The script's **full output**, verbatim.\n3. Per section: PASS / FAIL / NOT RUN, and for anything other than PASS, the\n   exact text.\n4. The manual detached-backfill result from section 4.\n5. For any suite failure, the section 5 attribution: pre-existing or regression.\n6. Anything you could not do and why.\n\n**Do not round a partial result up.** \"Section 3 ran its negative half\" is a\nuseful report. \"Section 3 passed\" implies the warning path was exercised, which\non most machines it was not — and that overstatement is how this change ends up\nshipping unverified.\nEOF\nwc -l \"$KIT/TESTING.md\"","description":"Write the main testing protocol"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":961,"cache_read_input_tokens":136131,"output_tokens":4208,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":19},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":961,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":4208,"cache_read_input_tokens":136131,"cache_creation_input_tokens":961,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":961},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"toolInputStreamMs":54752,"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01PUp2LCtWBPME2gKC8fXwuZ":{"command":"set -e\nKIT=/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-suhaan-devenv/93ae8948-c612-4072-8e70-2750e80df9ce/scratchpad/winkit\ncat > \"$KIT/TESTING.md\" <<'EOF'\n# Windows verification protocol\n\nRun this on a **real Windows machine**. Not WSL, not a Linux container, not Wine.\nWSL is a Linux kernel and will pass checks that Windows fails; it is the exact\nfalse green this kit exists to avoid.\n\nBudget about **20 minutes**, most of it the full test suite in section 5.\n\n---\n\n## 0. Prerequisites\n\n| Need | Check | If missing |\n|---|---|---|\n| Windows 10 or 11, native | — | stop, this kit is useless elsewhere |\n| PowerShell 5.1 or `pwsh` 7+ | `$PSVersionTable.PSVersion` | ships with Windows |\n| Go 1.26+ | `go version` | https://go.dev/dl/ — stop if absent |\n| Git for Windows | `git --version` | needed: one command under test is repo-scoped |\n\n`go version` must report `windows/amd64` or `windows/arm64`. If it says `linux`,\nyou are in WSL — open a real PowerShell instead.\n\nOptional, and it changes what section 1 can prove: the **host `entire` CLI** on\nPATH (`entire --version`). With it, section 1 runs the strong check. Without it,\nsection 1 falls back to a weaker one and says so. Do not install it just for\nthis; just report which you got.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Get the code\n\n```powershell\ngit clone <this repo's URL> brain-win\ncd brain-win\n```\n\nThe default branch holds the fix. Confirm you have three commits and that the\nmiddle two are the change under test:\n\n```powershell\ngit log --oneline\n```\n\nExpect, newest first:\n\n```\n<sha>  docs(limits): record the Windows path-length ceiling and narrow a lock claim\n<sha>  fix(windows): make the brain agree with the host on Windows\n<sha>  baseline: entire-brain origin/main @ 8cab9ba6\n```\n\nThe `baseline` tag points at that third commit. It is an untouched export of\nupstream `main` and exists for exactly one purpose: section 6.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Run the script\n\n```powershell\npwsh -File windows-verify.ps1\n```\n\nor, on PowerShell 5.1:\n\n```powershell\npowershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File windows-verify.ps1\n```\n\nIt refuses to run outside the repo root, and refuses to run without `go` and\n`git`, rather than failing confusingly three sections in. It prints `PASS` /\n`FAIL` / `..` per check and a summary at the end.\n\n**Capture the entire output.** The simplest way:\n\n```powershell\npwsh -File windows-verify.ps1 2>&1 | Tee-Object -FilePath verify-output.txt\n```\n\n---\n\n## 3. What each section actually proves\n\nThis is the part that matters more than the PASS lines. Several checks are\nweaker than their name suggests, and one thing under test is not covered at all.\n\n### Section 1 — store path\n\n**The defect:** the brain resolved its data directory through XDG and\n`~/.local/share` on every OS, while the host CLI ignores XDG on Windows and uses\n`%LOCALAPPDATA%`. A dispatched run was fine, because the host sets\n`ENTIRE_PLUGIN_DATA_DIR` explicitly. A **standalone** `entire-brain.exe` read a\ndifferent, empty store and said nothing about it.\n\n- **Strong check**, only if the host `entire` CLI is present: standalone and\n  dispatched runs must resolve the *same* store path. If they differ, the fix\n  did not work — that is the bug verbatim.\n- **Weak check**, the fallback: the resolved path merely sits under\n  `%LOCALAPPDATA%`. That is necessary but not sufficient.\n\nThe script clears `ENTIRE_PLUGIN_*_DIR` first, deliberately: the fix only fires\nwhen `ENTIRE_PLUGIN_DATA_DIR` is unset, so a test that sets it tests nothing.\n\n### Section 2 — `ProcessAlive`\n\n**The defect:** `os.FindProcess` succeeds for a process that has already exited\nwhile a handle to it is still open — and `os.FindProcess` itself holds exactly\nsuch a handle. So a finished backfill reported as *running* forever, and the\nguard that prevents a second backfill would never let one start again.\n\nRuns `TestProcessAlive` in `internal/cli/internal/brainstore`. These tests are\nWindows-only; on any other OS they do not exist, which is why they have never\nrun. Green here is real signal.\n\n### Section 3 — doctor warning for synced/UNC stores\n\n**The defect:** `%USERPROFILE%` is commonly OneDrive-redirected, and SQLite WAL\non a synced directory corrupts. Brain uses WAL and said nothing.\n\n**This section is conditional and will usually prove the negative half only.**\nIf your profile is *not* OneDrive-redirected, it checks that doctor does **not**\nwarn — real, but it is the no-false-positive half. The warning path itself only\ngets exercised on a machine whose store genuinely lands under OneDrive or a UNC\nshare. If yours does, say so loudly in your report; that is the valuable case.\n\n### Section 4 — long paths\n\n**The measurement:** the export tree's path *below the store root* reaches 277\ncharacters, and classic Win32 caps a path at 260 including the root. On paper\nevery deep export path is over the limit. It works only because Go rewrites long\n**absolute** paths to the `\\\\?\\` extended form before calling Win32 — which\ncovers Go's own `os` package and nothing else.\n\nSo this section writes a 277-char-shaped path under `%TEMP%` and reports whether\nit succeeded, plus your machine's `LongPathsEnabled` registry value.\n\n- A **PASS with `LongPathsEnabled` absent or 0** is the interesting result: it\n  confirms the margin is zero and that a Go implementation detail is the only\n  thing holding it up.\n- A **FAIL** means deep export paths are a live user bug on this machine. Report\n  the character count and the exact error.\n\nDo **not** enable `LongPathsEnabled` to make this pass. You would be measuring a\ndifferent machine.\n\n### Section 5 — full test suite\n\n`go test ./...` across the module. This is roughly what the project's Windows CI\nleg runs. It is the regression net: it proves the changes did not break the\n~42 packages that already worked.\n\nIt also compiles `*_windows.go` files that nothing else here executes — notably\nthe atomic-replace retry (see the gap below).\n\n### Not covered by the script at all\n\n- **Detached backfill** — needs a human. Section 4 of this document below.\n- **Atomic-replace retry on sharing violations** (`internal/config/atomic_replace_windows.go`).\n  Nothing in this kit reproduces a `SHARING_VIOLATION`; it needs a second\n  process holding the file open at the moment of replace. The suite proves it\n  compiles and does not regress, not that the retry fires. **Honest gap. Do not\n  report it as verified.**\n- **The windows/arm64 CI leg** (`.github/workflows/test.yml`). Only a CI run can\n  prove that, and nothing here pushes. Unverifiable in this kit by design.\n\n---\n\n## 4. The manual check (please do this one)\n\nThe script cannot test it, and it is the most user-visible fix in the set.\n\n**The defect:** `CREATE_NEW_PROCESS_GROUP` alone does not detach a child from the\nconsole. Closing the terminal killed the background backfill — the single thing\nthe detached spawn exists to prevent.\n\n```powershell\n# in some git repository on this machine, NOT this kit\nentire brain setup\n```\n\nThen, **while the background backfill is still running**, close the terminal\nwindow — click the X, do not Ctrl-C and do not `exit`.\n\nCheck two things:\n\n1. **Open Task Manager** (or `Get-Process entire-brain`). `entire-brain.exe`\n   should **still be running**. Before the fix it died with the console.\n2. **No console window should have flashed** on screen when the backfill started.\n\nReport: did the process survive, and did a window flash? If you cannot run\n`entire brain setup` because the host CLI is not installed, say so — that is a\nlegitimate \"not run\", not a failure.\n\n> Deliberately **not** `CREATE_NO_WINDOW`: Microsoft documents that flag as\n> undefined in combination with `DETACHED_PROCESS`. If you see a brief console\n> flash, report it rather than assuming it is the bug — it may be the documented\n> tradeoff.\n\n---\n\n## 5. If the full suite fails\n\nDo not report a suite failure as a regression until you have checked whether it\nis pre-existing. The `baseline` tag is there for this.\n\n```powershell\n# note the failing package from the section 5 output, e.g. ./internal/cli\ngo test ./internal/cli -count=1          # on the fix: should reproduce\ngit checkout baseline\ngo test ./internal/cli -count=1          # on untouched upstream\ngit checkout -                           # back to the fix\n```\n\n- Fails on **both** → pre-existing upstream failure, **not** caused by this\n  change. Still worth reporting, labelled as pre-existing.\n- Fails only on the **fix** → a real regression. This is the highest-value\n  finding in the whole exercise. Report the full test output.\n\nRun the suite with `-count=1` to defeat Go's test cache, and if a failure looks\nlike it might be load- or timing-related, re-run that one package alone before\ncalling it.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Things that are **not** bugs\n\nDo not report these as failures.\n\n- **`..` lines in the output.** Those are informational, not failures. Only\n  `FAIL` counts, and the summary lists them.\n- **\"host 'entire' CLI not on PATH; skipped...\"** — expected if you did not\n  install it. Section 1 just ran its weaker half.\n- **\"profile is not OneDrive-redirected\"** — expected on most machines. Section 3\n  ran its negative half.\n- **Section 4 passing with `LongPathsEnabled` empty or 0.** That is the expected\n  and most informative outcome.\n- **Capture hooks doing nothing.** This kit ships `.entire/settings.json` with\n  `enabled: false` and has `.claude/settings.json` removed, on purpose: the real\n  file invokes hooks via `sh -c`, and in a throwaway clone it would attribute\n  checkpoints to the wrong repository. Both are kit-local changes and are **not**\n  part of the fix under test.\n\n### One thing worth noticing while you are there\n\nThe real repo's `.claude/settings.json` (removed here, visible in the `baseline`\ncommit) wires every hook as `sh -c '...'`. Git for Windows installs `sh.exe`\noutside the default PATH, so on a machine without Git Bash on PATH those hooks\nfail at every hook event. **If you have a moment**, check whether `sh` resolves\non your machine:\n\n```powershell\nGet-Command sh -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue\n```\n\nReport the answer. It is not part of this change, but it is a plausible Windows\ndefect in the project and nobody has checked it on real hardware.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Report back\n\nFill in `RESULTS-TEMPLATE.md` and send it back. The things that must be in it:\n\n1. Windows version, `go version` output, `$PSVersionTable.PSVersion`, and\n   whether the host `entire` CLI was present.\n2. The script's **full output**, verbatim.\n3. Per section: PASS / FAIL / NOT RUN, and for anything other than PASS, the\n   exact text.\n4. The manual detached-backfill result from section 4.\n5. For any suite failure, the section 5 attribution: pre-existing or regression.\n6. Anything you could not do and why.\n\n**Do not round a partial result up.** \"Section 3 ran its negative half\" is a\nuseful report. \"Section 3 passed\" implies the warning path was exercised, which\non most machines it was not — and that overstatement is how this change ends up\nshipping unverified.\nEOF\nwc -l \"$KIT/TESTING.md\"","description":"Write the main testing protocol"}},"serverClassifierRequest":"056e8e1b-0024-4bea-b754-1c28ba40da0e","apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011Ch7kWybM7KL32KxoNBH8P","requestRef":"3c69ac0a-f6ac-4c13-816e-dc2db6c4d8b3","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5","promptCacheRequested":"1h","type":"assistant","uuid":"efc2b3eb-5bff-4f8f-9497-fc1761a3551f","timestamp":"2026-11-17T02:59:31.069Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"xhigh","perTurnEffort":null,"session_id":"1041b1a0-c015-41c2-a29f-4f2db2f6e0cb","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv","sessionId":"93ae8948-c612-4072-8e70-2750e80df9ce","version":"2.1.323","gitBranch":"main","slug":"floofy-discovering-bachman"}
