{"parentUuid":"f97afc03-8886-4ca2-8390-b724d85deeca","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"cd77a49b-0310-4c19-83fd-cbf2eff562e1","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012gCLvTnbe7guajywFKad7n","type":"tool_result","content":"################ README.md ################\n# entire-brain Windows findings\n\nResults of running `entire-brain-windows-verify` on a real Windows 11 machine,\n2026-11-16, packaged for an agent to fix.\n\n**Start with `FIX-BRIEF.md`.** It lists what to fix, in priority order, with\nfile and line pointers, a suggested direction and what \"done\" means.\n\n| Path | What it is |\n|---|---|\n| `FIX-BRIEF.md` | The task: 2 defects in the fix (F1, F2), 5 pre-existing bugs (P1–P5), 5 kit fixes (K1–K5), how to verify |\n| `RESULTS.md` | The full verification report, in the kit's template: environment, script output, per-section verdicts, attribution of every failing test |\n| `patch/fix-under-test.diff` | `git diff baseline..bada920`, exactly the two fix commits |\n| `patch/commits.txt` | Commit list and `--stat` for the fix and for the kit commit |\n| `evidence/verify-output.txt` | Full output of `windows-verify.ps1` (one block redacted, see below) |\n| `evidence/attribution-output.txt` | The 26 failing tests re-run on HEAD, `bada920` and `baseline`, plus a `GIT_CEILING_DIRECTORIES` diagnostic pass |\n| `evidence/attribution2-output.txt` | The 23 tests that only surfaced after the timeout, on `bada920` and `baseline`, plus the store-path tests with the real `LOCALAPPDATA` |\n| `evidence/cli-full-main.txt` | Full `go test ./internal/cli -v -timeout 30m` on HEAD (diagnostic environment, described in the header line) |\n| `evidence/env-facts.txt` | Machine facts captured in the shell that ran the kit |\n| `evidence/store-leak-listing.txt` | The real brain store that tests created under `%LOCALAPPDATA%` (F1) |\n| `evidence/home-folder-writes.txt` | What tests wrote into the user's home folder and global Claude settings (P1) |\n| `repro/` | The wrapper scripts used for every run, and the Go long-path probe |\n\nRedaction: the `tools/coverage` test prints `git status` of the enclosing repo.\nHere that's the user's home folder, so its output lists the user's personal file\nnames. Those lines are replaced with a marker in every file in this bundle.\nNothing else is edited.\n\nNo product code was changed during the run, nothing was committed, and nothing was pushed.\n\n################ FIX-BRIEF.md ################\n# Fix brief: entire-brain Windows fix, verified on real Windows\n\nYou are fixing defects found by running the Windows verification kit on a real\nWindows 11 machine. The fix under test is two commits on top of\n`entireio/entire-brain` main @ `8cab9ba6`:\n\n- `fe4bc34` fix(windows): make the brain agree with the host on Windows\n- `bada920` docs(limits): record the Windows path-length ceiling and narrow a lock claim\n\n`patch/fix-under-test.diff` is exactly `git diff baseline..bada920`. Work in the\ncheckout that carries those two commits, **not** in the throwaway kit repo\n(`entire-brain-windows-verify`). Do not push, open PRs, or touch\n`entireio/entire-brain` unless the owner tells you to.\n\nEvidence is in `RESULTS.md` (the full report) and `evidence/`. Every claim\nbelow cites the test or log it comes from. Root causes marked *(from reading)*\ncame from reading the code and were not proven by an experiment. Confirm them\nbefore you rely on them.\n\n---\n\n## Must fix: defects in the change under test\n\n### F1. Windows store-path fix breaks test isolation (regression)\n\n**Fails on the fix, passes on `baseline`:**\n- `internal/cli` `TestTestMainPinsXDGRoots`\n- `internal/cli` `TestDoctorUsesXDGFallbacks`\n- `internal/cli` `TestDefaultStoreFallbackStaysInsideTheSandbox`\n\nThe failures reproduce with the real `%LOCALAPPDATA%` and with it redirected\n(`evidence/attribution2-output.txt`, last block).\n\n```\nmain_test.go:44: default plugin directory \"C:\\\\Users\\\\<user>\\\\AppData\\\\Local\\\\entire\\\\plugins\\\\data\\\\brain\" escaped temporary root \"...\\\\entire-brain-cli-test-xdg-2343469481\"\ntesthome_test.go:115: ENTIRE_PLUGIN_DATA_DIR resolved to \"C:\\\\Users\\\\<user>\\\\AppData\\\\Local\\\\entire\\\\plugins\\\\data\\\\brain\", outside the redirected home \"...\\\\001\"\nroot_test.go:348: doctor output missing \"plugin data dir: ok (...\\\\001\\\\data\\\\entire\\\\plugins\\\\data\\\\brain\"\n```\n\n**Root cause** *(from reading)*:\n- `internal/cli/internal/base/env.go:172` `resolvePluginDataRoot` deliberately ignores\n  `XDG_DATA_HOME` on Windows when `ENTIRE_PLUGIN_DATA_DIR` is unset. It reads\n  `%LOCALAPPDATA%`, then `%USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local`.\n- The two test sandboxes don't cover that:\n  - `internal/cli/internal/clitest/clitest.go:27` `PinXDGRoots`, the `TestMain` of\n    ~25 packages, pins only the four `XDG_*` variables.\n  - `internal/cli/testhome_test.go` `useDefaultStoreFallback` / `redirectHomeDir`\n    redirects `HOME`/`USERPROFILE` but not `LOCALAPPDATA`.\n- So on Windows every test that resolves the default data dir now resolves the\n  developer's **real** store. The clitest package comment (\"Native Go test runs\n  use the same isolated storage on Linux, macOS, and Windows\") is false on\n  Windows after this change.\n\n**Observed harm:** test processes created and populated a real store at\n`%LOCALAPPDATA%\\entire\\plugins\\data\\brain\\repos\\local\\<user>-2a1c2c75cab9`\n(`evidence/store-leak-listing.txt`). On `baseline` nothing escaped.\n\n**Direction:**\n- Make both sandboxes cover the Windows branch. `PinXDGRoots` should also pin\n  `LOCALAPPDATA` under its temp root. `redirectHomeDir`/`useDefaultStoreFallback`\n  should redirect `LOCALAPPDATA` together with `HOME`/`USERPROFILE`. Pin it on every\n  OS, so a macOS/Linux run with `goos=\"windows\"` passed explicitly is also\n  sandboxed.\n- `TestDoctorUsesXDGFallbacks` asserts the data dir follows XDG. On Windows\n  that's now intentionally wrong (the host ignores XDG there; see CHANGES.md\n  defect 1). Make the expectation OS-aware rather than reverting the behaviour.\n- Keep `TestTestMainPinsXDGRoots` and `TestDefaultStoreFallbackStaysInsideTheSandbox`\n  as the guards. They're correct; the sandbox is what's incomplete.\n- Grep for any other place that sandboxes the default store by XDG alone.\n\n**Done when:** on Windows, all three tests pass, and running `go test ./internal/cli`\ncreates nothing under the real `%LOCALAPPDATA%\\entire`.\n\n### F2. OneDrive warning can never fire on Windows (defect in a new test's subject)\n\n**Fails on Windows** (new test, so there's no baseline): `internal/cli`\n`TestSyncedStoreDirDetailFlagsOneDriveOnWindows`, all 3 subtests:\n`doctor_synced_store_test.go:44: want a warning, got none`. It's a pure function\nwith an injected environment, so the result doesn't depend on the machine.\n\n**Root cause** *(from reading, consistent with the failure)*:\n`internal/cli/doctor_synced_store.go:84` `pathIsUnder` normalizes with\n`filepath.Clean(strings.ReplaceAll(p, \"\\\\\", \"/\"))`.\n- On Windows, `filepath.Clean` turns `/` back into `\\`, and the code then checks\n  `strings.HasPrefix(p, r+\"/\")`, which never matches.\n- On macOS, `filepath.Clean` keeps `/`, which is why the test was green there.\n- UNC detection (`isUNCPath`) doesn't use `pathIsUnder` and is unaffected.\n\n**Direction:** make normalization OS-independent, for example use package `path`\n(not `filepath`) on the slash-converted, lower-cased string. Then this code\nbehaves identically on every OS, and the existing tests become real cross-OS\ntests. Add a test with backslash paths plus a sibling-prefix case\n(`OneDrive-Archive`) that would have failed on macOS before the fix.\n\n**Done when:** on Windows, `TestSyncedStoreDirDetailFlagsOneDriveOnWindows` and\n`TestSyncedStoreDirDetailIgnoresSiblingsOfTheOneDriveRoot` both pass.\n\n---\n\n## Should fix: pre-existing bugs (also fail on `baseline`) that this run exposed\n\nThese are not regressions, but three of them did real damage to a developer\nmachine during a plain `go test ./...`.\n\n### P1. Tests write agent setup into the enclosing repo, which can be the user's home folder\n\nOn this machine `C:\\Users\\<user>` is itself a Git repository, so every `%TEMP%`\ntest directory sits inside a repo. During the runs, tests resolved the home\nfolder as the repo root and wrote:\n- `C:\\Users\\<user>\\CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md` and `.entire\\agent-guide.md`. These\n  were written during the **`baseline`** targeted `internal/cli` run\n  (23:09:20–23:10:05; the files are timestamped 23:09:53).\n- A `PreToolUse` hook merged into **`C:\\Users\\<user>\\.claude\\settings.json`, which is\n  the user's global Claude Code settings**:\n  `cmd /c \"entire-brain hook pre-edit --claude-hook || entire brain hook pre-edit --claude-hook || exit /b 0\"`.\n  It was written at 22:58:53 by the stray worker chain (P2) running the fix code.\n\nDetails: `evidence/home-folder-writes.txt`. Which test does it isn't isolated;\nthe likely one is `TestNestedRepoWarningUsesTheQuotedForm`, which drives a full\nsetup and fails here with \"the nested-repo warning did not fire\".\n\n**Two separate problems:**\n1. **Tests aren't hermetic.** Repo discovery from a temp dir walks up into an\n   unrelated repo. `GIT_CEILING_DIRECTORIES` fixes some tests (see the table in\n   RESULTS.md), but `TestCoordinationOutsideRepository`,\n   `TestOuterRepoIsEmptyForATopLevelRepo` and `TestOuterRepoRequiresAnInnerRepository`\n   still see `C:\\Users\\<user>` as an outer repo, so that lookup doesn't use or\n   honour git's ceiling (go-git, or a custom walk).\n2. **Product risk.** `entire brain setup` in a repo rooted at the home folder\n   writes `<home>\\.claude\\settings.json`, which is global config, not project\n   config. Consider refusing, or at least warning, when the repo root is the\n   user's home directory.\n\n### P2. Under `go test`, the memory worker re-execs the test binary and forms a chain\n\n`internal/cli/memory_cmd.go:144` `launchDetachedSelf` runs\n`os.Executable() memory worker --once`. Under `go test` that's `cli.test.exe`,\nwhich treats `memory worker --once` as positional arguments and reruns the\n**whole package**. That rerun reaches the launcher again.\n\nWhat happened here: 34 chained `cli.test.exe` processes, each the child of the\nprevious one, one new process every 15–75 s. The chain outlived the test run,\ncaused the 10-minute package timeout (the package passes its own timing in 376 s\nwithout it), held `cli.test.exe` open (\"unlinkat ... Access is denied\"), and ran\nthe full test suite in the home-repo context (the source of P1). It also\nhappens on `baseline`. On this machine it's triggered by the home-repo condition\n(`TestPlainDirectoryIsNotMistakenForARepository` fails: \"a plain directory was\ntreated as a repository\").\n\n**Direction:** make real self-relaunch impossible under test. Options: stub\n`memoryWorkerLaunch`/`memoryWorkerLaunchAfter` in every `TestMain`; have\n`launchDetachedSelf` refuse when `testing.Testing()` is true; or give the test\nbinary a helper-mode guard. Any of these is defence in depth. The unstubbed path\nmust fail closed.\n\n### P3. 32 tests fail instead of skipping when symlinks aren't permitted\n\n`os.Symlink` returns `A required privilege is not held by the client` for a\nnon-elevated user without Developer Mode. 3 `internal/agentsetup` tests and 29\n`internal/cli` tests fail on that error instead of skipping (list in RESULTS.md).\nSkip on `ERROR_PRIVILEGE_NOT_HELD`, the same way the suite already handles other\nplatform limits.\n\n### P4. The repository can't be checked out with stock Git for Windows\n\n13 tracked paths exceed 260 characters at a 48-character clone root. The longest\nrelative path is 215 characters, under\n`benchmarks/agent-brain/evidence/release/release-candidate-entire-cli-mcp-manual-attribution-clean-20260611T0412Z/...`.\n`git clone` fails with \"Filename too long\" unless `core.longpaths=true`. Same on\n`baseline`. Shorten those evidence paths, or document the setting in the\ncontributor docs.\n\n### P5. Undiagnosed\n`TestDegenerateRepositoriesAreNamedRatherThanLeakingGitText` and\n`TestWorktreeRefreshNamesADegenerateRepository` fail on fix and baseline: the\n`bare_repository` / `not_a_repository` subtests don't name the condition. The\nlikely cause is the home-repo environment, but it wasn't proven.\n\n---\n\n## Kit fixes (the `entire-brain-windows-verify` repo, not the product)\n\n- **K1.** The kit commit replaces `README.md`, which by itself fails 5 tests:\n  `TestREADMEDocumentsBothActivationsAndTheirOrder`, `TestDocContractCapabilityMatrix`,\n  `TestEveryVisibleCommandIsDocumented`, `TestDocumentedCommandContractIsBidirectional`\n  and `TestFeatureMatrixProseMatchesTheData`. All pass on `bada920`. Keep the\n  project README and put the kit's text in another file.\n- **K2.** TESTING.md expects 3 commits; there are 4. CHANGES.md says\n  `git diff baseline..HEAD` \"is exactly this\", but HEAD includes the kit commit.\n  The exact diff is `baseline..bada920`.\n- **K3.** Section 4 writes through PowerShell 5.1, not Go, so it measures \"tools\n  outside Go\", not the brain. `New-Item -Force` fails silently at 368 characters:\n  no exception, and no directory is created. A Go probe (`repro/gprobe/`) wrote\n  383 characters with `LongPathsEnabled=0`. Probe with Go, and check\n  `Test-Path` after `New-Item`.\n- **K4.** `windows-verify.ps1` lets the stray-worker chain run unchecked. After\n  section 5, count and report leftover `cli.test.exe` processes, and run the\n  suite with `GIT_CEILING_DIRECTORIES` set to the parent of the clone.\n- **K5.** Preflight should detect that `%USERPROFILE%` (or any ancestor of\n  `%TEMP%`) is a Git repo and warn, since that changes many results.\n\n---\n\n## Verify (has to be on Windows)\n\nmacOS can't reproduce F1, F2, P2 or P3; that's why they shipped. After fixing:\n\n1. Add cross-OS tests wherever the logic allows it (F2 especially).\n2. Hand the branch back to the owner to re-run on the Windows machine. The\n   scripts in `repro/` re-run the kit, the per-test attribution and the full\n   `internal/cli` package. They contain this machine's absolute paths; adjust\n   `$session` and the clone path at the top of each.\n3. Acceptance on Windows:\n   - F1 and F2 tests green\n   - no `cli.test.exe` left after `go test ./internal/cli`\n   - nothing new under real `%LOCALAPPDATA%\\entire` or the home folder\n   - remaining failures limited to P3/P5 (or fixed)\n\nStill unverified and out of reach here: the strong section 1 check and the\nmanual detached-backfill check (both need the host `entire` CLI, which wasn't\ninstalled), the atomic-replace retry (no `SHARING_VIOLATION` reproduction), and\nthe windows/arm64 CI leg.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"df33b2cb-9e01-4e44-bb97-3f6c10e29f01","timestamp":"2026-11-17T21:15:11.962Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"################ README.md ################\n# entire-brain Windows findings\n\nResults of running `entire-brain-windows-verify` on a real Windows 11 machine,\n2026-11-16, packaged for an agent to fix.\n\n**Start with `FIX-BRIEF.md`.** It lists what to fix, in priority order, with\nfile and line pointers, a suggested direction and what \"done\" means.\n\n| Path | What it is |\n|---|---|\n| `FIX-BRIEF.md` | The task: 2 defects in the fix (F1, F2), 5 pre-existing bugs (P1–P5), 5 kit fixes (K1–K5), how to verify |\n| `RESULTS.md` | The full verification report, in the kit's template: environment, script output, per-section verdicts, attribution of every failing test |\n| `patch/fix-under-test.diff` | `git diff baseline..bada920`, exactly the two fix commits |\n| `patch/commits.txt` | Commit list and `--stat` for the fix and for the kit commit |\n| `evidence/verify-output.txt` | Full output of `windows-verify.ps1` (one block redacted, see below) |\n| `evidence/attribution-output.txt` | The 26 failing tests re-run on HEAD, `bada920` and `baseline`, plus a `GIT_CEILING_DIRECTORIES` diagnostic pass |\n| `evidence/attribution2-output.txt` | The 23 tests that only surfaced after the timeout, on `bada920` and `baseline`, plus the store-path tests with the real `LOCALAPPDATA` |\n| `evidence/cli-full-main.txt` | Full `go test ./internal/cli -v -timeout 30m` on HEAD (diagnostic environment, described in the header line) |\n| `evidence/env-facts.txt` | Machine facts captured in the shell that ran the kit |\n| `evidence/store-leak-listing.txt` | The real brain store that tests created under `%LOCALAPPDATA%` (F1) |\n| `evidence/home-folder-writes.txt` | What tests wrote into the user's home folder and global Claude settings (P1) |\n| `repro/` | The wrapper scripts used for every run, and the Go long-path probe |\n\nRedaction: the `tools/coverage` test prints `git status` of the enclosing repo.\nHere that's the user's home folder, so its output lists the user's personal file\nnames. Those lines are replaced with a marker in every file in this bundle.\nNothing else is edited.\n\nNo product code was changed during the run, nothing was committed, and nothing was pushed.\n\n################ FIX-BRIEF.md ################\n# Fix brief: entire-brain Windows fix, verified on real Windows\n\nYou are fixing defects found by running the Windows verification kit on a real\nWindows 11 machine. The fix under test is two commits on top of\n`entireio/entire-brain` main @ `8cab9ba6`:\n\n- `fe4bc34` fix(windows): make the brain agree with the host on Windows\n- `bada920` docs(limits): record the Windows path-length ceiling and narrow a lock claim\n\n`patch/fix-under-test.diff` is exactly `git diff baseline..bada920`. Work in the\ncheckout that carries those two commits, **not** in the throwaway kit repo\n(`entire-brain-windows-verify`). Do not push, open PRs, or touch\n`entireio/entire-brain` unless the owner tells you to.\n\nEvidence is in `RESULTS.md` (the full report) and `evidence/`. Every claim\nbelow cites the test or log it comes from. Root causes marked *(from reading)*\ncame from reading the code and were not proven by an experiment. Confirm them\nbefore you rely on them.\n\n---\n\n## Must fix: defects in the change under test\n\n### F1. Windows store-path fix breaks test isolation (regression)\n\n**Fails on the fix, passes on `baseline`:**\n- `internal/cli` `TestTestMainPinsXDGRoots`\n- `internal/cli` `TestDoctorUsesXDGFallbacks`\n- `internal/cli` `TestDefaultStoreFallbackStaysInsideTheSandbox`\n\nThe failures reproduce with the real `%LOCALAPPDATA%` and with it redirected\n(`evidence/attribution2-output.txt`, last block).\n\n```\nmain_test.go:44: default plugin directory \"C:\\\\Users\\\\<user>\\\\AppData\\\\Local\\\\entire\\\\plugins\\\\data\\\\brain\" escaped temporary root \"...\\\\entire-brain-cli-test-xdg-2343469481\"\ntesthome_test.go:115: ENTIRE_PLUGIN_DATA_DIR resolved to \"C:\\\\Users\\\\<user>\\\\AppData\\\\Local\\\\entire\\\\plugins\\\\data\\\\brain\", outside the redirected home \"...\\\\001\"\nroot_test.go:348: doctor output missing \"plugin data dir: ok (...\\\\001\\\\data\\\\entire\\\\plugins\\\\data\\\\brain\"\n```\n\n**Root cause** *(from reading)*:\n- `internal/cli/internal/base/env.go:172` `resolvePluginDataRoot` deliberately ignores\n  `XDG_DATA_HOME` on Windows when `ENTIRE_PLUGIN_DATA_DIR` is unset. It reads\n  `%LOCALAPPDATA%`, then `%USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local`.\n- The two test sandboxes don't cover that:\n  - `internal/cli/internal/clitest/clitest.go:27` `PinXDGRoots`, the `TestMain` of\n    ~25 packages, pins only the four `XDG_*` variables.\n  - `internal/cli/testhome_test.go` `useDefaultStoreFallback` / `redirectHomeDir`\n    redirects `HOME`/`USERPROFILE` but not `LOCALAPPDATA`.\n- So on Windows every test that resolves the default data dir now resolves the\n  developer's **real** store. The clitest package comment (\"Native Go test runs\n  use the same isolated storage on Linux, macOS, and Windows\") is false on\n  Windows after this change.\n\n**Observed harm:** test processes created and populated a real store at\n`%LOCALAPPDATA%\\entire\\plugins\\data\\brain\\repos\\local\\<user>-2a1c2c75cab9`\n(`evidence/store-leak-listing.txt`). On `baseline` nothing escaped.\n\n**Direction:**\n- Make both sandboxes cover the Windows branch. `PinXDGRoots` should also pin\n  `LOCALAPPDATA` under its temp root. `redirectHomeDir`/`useDefaultStoreFallback`\n  should redirect `LOCALAPPDATA` together with `HOME`/`USERPROFILE`. Pin it on every\n  OS, so a macOS/Linux run with `goos=\"windows\"` passed explicitly is also\n  sandboxed.\n- `TestDoctorUsesXDGFallbacks` asserts the data dir follows XDG. On Windows\n  that's now intentionally wrong (the host ignores XDG there; see CHANGES.md\n  defect 1). Make the expectation OS-aware rather than reverting the behaviour.\n- Keep `TestTestMainPinsXDGRoots` and `TestDefaultStoreFallbackStaysInsideTheSandbox`\n  as the guards. They're correct; the sandbox is what's incomplete.\n- Grep for any other place that sandboxes the default store by XDG alone.\n\n**Done when:** on Windows, all three tests pass, and running `go test ./internal/cli`\ncreates nothing under the real `%LOCALAPPDATA%\\entire`.\n\n### F2. OneDrive warning can never fire on Windows (defect in a new test's subject)\n\n**Fails on Windows** (new test, so there's no baseline): `internal/cli`\n`TestSyncedStoreDirDetailFlagsOneDriveOnWindows`, all 3 subtests:\n`doctor_synced_store_test.go:44: want a warning, got none`. It's a pure function\nwith an injected environment, so the result doesn't depend on the machine.\n\n**Root cause** *(from reading, consistent with the failure)*:\n`internal/cli/doctor_synced_store.go:84` `pathIsUnder` normalizes with\n`filepath.Clean(strings.ReplaceAll(p, \"\\\\\", \"/\"))`.\n- On Windows, `filepath.Clean` turns `/` back into `\\`, and the code then checks\n  `strings.HasPrefix(p, r+\"/\")`, which never matches.\n- On macOS, `filepath.Clean` keeps `/`, which is why the test was green there.\n- UNC detection (`isUNCPath`) doesn't use `pathIsUnder` and is unaffected.\n\n**Direction:** make normalization OS-independent, for example use package `path`\n(not `filepath`) on the slash-converted, lower-cased string. Then this code\nbehaves identically on every OS, and the existing tests become real cross-OS\ntests. Add a test with backslash paths plus a sibling-prefix case\n(`OneDrive-Archive`) that would have failed on macOS before the fix.\n\n**Done when:** on Windows, `TestSyncedStoreDirDetailFlagsOneDriveOnWindows` and\n`TestSyncedStoreDirDetailIgnoresSiblingsOfTheOneDriveRoot` both pass.\n\n---\n\n## Should fix: pre-existing bugs (also fail on `baseline`) that this run exposed\n\nThese are not regressions, but three of them did real damage to a developer\nmachine during a plain `go test ./...`.\n\n### P1. Tests write agent setup into the enclosing repo, which can be the user's home folder\n\nOn this machine `C:\\Users\\<user>` is itself a Git repository, so every `%TEMP%`\ntest directory sits inside a repo. During the runs, tests resolved the home\nfolder as the repo root and wrote:\n- `C:\\Users\\<user>\\CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md` and `.entire\\agent-guide.md`. These\n  were written during the **`baseline`** targeted `internal/cli` run\n  (23:09:20–23:10:05; the files are timestamped 23:09:53).\n- A `PreToolUse` hook merged into **`C:\\Users\\<user>\\.claude\\settings.json`, which is\n  the user's global Claude Code settings**:\n  `cmd /c \"entire-brain hook pre-edit --claude-hook || entire brain hook pre-edit --claude-hook || exit /b 0\"`.\n  It was written at 22:58:53 by the stray worker chain (P2) running the fix code.\n\nDetails: `evidence/home-folder-writes.txt`. Which test does it isn't isolated;\nthe likely one is `TestNestedRepoWarningUsesTheQuotedForm`, which drives a full\nsetup and fails here with \"the nested-repo warning did not fire\".\n\n**Two separate problems:**\n1. **Tests aren't hermetic.** Repo discovery from a temp dir walks up into an\n   unrelated repo. `GIT_CEILING_DIRECTORIES` fixes some tests (see the table in\n   RESULTS.md), but `TestCoordinationOutsideRepository`,\n   `TestOuterRepoIsEmptyForATopLevelRepo` and `TestOuterRepoRequiresAnInnerRepository`\n   still see `C:\\Users\\<user>` as an outer repo, so that lookup doesn't use or\n   honour git's ceiling (go-git, or a custom walk).\n2. **Product risk.** `entire brain setup` in a repo rooted at the home folder\n   writes `<home>\\.claude\\settings.json`, which is global config, not project\n   config. Consider refusing, or at least warning, when the repo root is the\n   user's home directory.\n\n### P2. Under `go test`, the memory worker re-execs the test binary and forms a chain\n\n`internal/cli/memory_cmd.go:144` `launchDetachedSelf` runs\n`os.Executable() memory worker --once`. Under `go test` that's `cli.test.exe`,\nwhich treats `memory worker --once` as positional arguments and reruns the\n**whole package**. That rerun reaches the launcher again.\n\nWhat happened here: 34 chained `cli.test.exe` processes, each the child of the\nprevious one, one new process every 15–75 s. The chain outlived the test run,\ncaused the 10-minute package timeout (the package passes its own timing in 376 s\nwithout it), held `cli.test.exe` open (\"unlinkat ... Access is denied\"), and ran\nthe full test suite in the home-repo context (the source of P1). It also\nhappens on `baseline`. On this machine it's triggered by the home-repo condition\n(`TestPlainDirectoryIsNotMistakenForARepository` fails: \"a plain directory was\ntreated as a repository\").\n\n**Direction:** make real self-relaunch impossible under test. Options: stub\n`memoryWorkerLaunch`/`memoryWorkerLaunchAfter` in every `TestMain`; have\n`launchDetachedSelf` refuse when `testing.Testing()` is true; or give the test\nbinary a helper-mode guard. Any of these is defence in depth. The unstubbed path\nmust fail closed.\n\n### P3. 32 tests fail instead of skipping when symlinks aren't permitted\n\n`os.Symlink` returns `A required privilege is not held by the client` for a\nnon-elevated user without Developer Mode. 3 `internal/agentsetup` tests and 29\n`internal/cli` tests fail on that error instead of skipping (list in RESULTS.md).\nSkip on `ERROR_PRIVILEGE_NOT_HELD`, the same way the suite already handles other\nplatform limits.\n\n### P4. The repository can't be checked out with stock Git for Windows\n\n13 tracked paths exceed 260 characters at a 48-character clone root. The longest\nrelative path is 215 characters, under\n`benchmarks/agent-brain/evidence/release/release-candidate-entire-cli-mcp-manual-attribution-clean-20260611T0412Z/...`.\n`git clone` fails with \"Filename too long\" unless `core.longpaths=true`. Same on\n`baseline`. Shorten those evidence paths, or document the setting in the\ncontributor docs.\n\n### P5. Undiagnosed\n`TestDegenerateRepositoriesAreNamedRatherThanLeakingGitText` and\n`TestWorktreeRefreshNamesADegenerateRepository` fail on fix and baseline: the\n`bare_repository` / `not_a_repository` subtests don't name the condition. The\nlikely cause is the home-repo environment, but it wasn't proven.\n\n---\n\n## Kit fixes (the `entire-brain-windows-verify` repo, not the product)\n\n- **K1.** The kit commit replaces `README.md`, which by itself fails 5 tests:\n  `TestREADMEDocumentsBothActivationsAndTheirOrder`, `TestDocContractCapabilityMatrix`,\n  `TestEveryVisibleCommandIsDocumented`, `TestDocumentedCommandContractIsBidirectional`\n  and `TestFeatureMatrixProseMatchesTheData`. All pass on `bada920`. Keep the\n  project README and put the kit's text in another file.\n- **K2.** TESTING.md expects 3 commits; there are 4. CHANGES.md says\n  `git diff baseline..HEAD` \"is exactly this\", but HEAD includes the kit commit.\n  The exact diff is `baseline..bada920`.\n- **K3.** Section 4 writes through PowerShell 5.1, not Go, so it measures \"tools\n  outside Go\", not the brain. `New-Item -Force` fails silently at 368 characters:\n  no exception, and no directory is created. A Go probe (`repro/gprobe/`) wrote\n  383 characters with `LongPathsEnabled=0`. Probe with Go, and check\n  `Test-Path` after `New-Item`.\n- **K4.** `windows-verify.ps1` lets the stray-worker chain run unchecked. After\n  section 5, count and report leftover `cli.test.exe` processes, and run the\n  suite with `GIT_CEILING_DIRECTORIES` set to the parent of the clone.\n- **K5.** Preflight should detect that `%USERPROFILE%` (or any ancestor of\n  `%TEMP%`) is a Git repo and warn, since that changes many results.\n\n---\n\n## Verify (has to be on Windows)\n\nmacOS can't reproduce F1, F2, P2 or P3; that's why they shipped. After fixing:\n\n1. Add cross-OS tests wherever the logic allows it (F2 especially).\n2. Hand the branch back to the owner to re-run on the Windows machine. The\n   scripts in `repro/` re-run the kit, the per-test attribution and the full\n   `internal/cli` package. They contain this machine's absolute paths; adjust\n   `$session` and the clone path at the top of each.\n3. Acceptance on Windows:\n   - F1 and F2 tests green\n   - no `cli.test.exe` left after `go test ./internal/cli`\n   - nothing new under real `%LOCALAPPDATA%\\entire` or the home folder\n   - remaining failures limited to P3/P5 (or fixed)\n\nStill unverified and out of reach here: the strong section 1 check and the\nmanual detached-backfill check (both need the host `entire` CLI, which wasn't\ninstalled), the atomic-replace retry (no `SHARING_VIOLATION` reproduction), and\nthe windows/arm64 CI leg.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"3bccdc47-92be-49fb-964b-980a8566d8f8","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv","root":"/Users/suhaan/devenv","branch":"main","default_branch":null,"status":{"clean":false,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":1,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":273},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remote":"entirehq/devenv","visibility":"private"},"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[{"name":"origin","host":"github.com","remote":"entirehq/devenv","visibility":"private"}],"visibility_cache":[{"host":"github.com","remote":"entirehq/devenv","visibility":"private"},{"host":"github.com","remote":"entireio/entire-brain","visibility":"public"}]}},"live_cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv","platform":"macos"}},"sandboxed":false,"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"f97afc03-8886-4ca2-8390-b724d85deeca","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"session_id":"1041b1a0-c015-41c2-a29f-4f2db2f6e0cb","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv","sessionId":"93ae8948-c612-4072-8e70-2750e80df9ce","version":"2.1.323","gitBranch":"main","slug":"floofy-discovering-bachman"}
