{"parentUuid":"7fe37168-e74b-458f-a34a-f331039dffbd","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011Ch9Pe4x4tJLaMsoYfTADk","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01FNvj1W7e1fZF1yK9dWbdQi","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"K3=/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-suhaan-devenv/93ae8948-c612-4072-8e70-2750e80df9ce/scratchpad/winkit3\ncat > \"$K3/GRAPH-NOTES.md\" <<'EOF'\n# entire-graph on Windows — the map\n\nRead this before `verify-graph.ps1`. Graph is in a **much better** Windows\nposition than brain was, and the useful work is therefore different: there is\nalmost nothing here about whether it compiles, and a lot about what a CI runner\nstructurally cannot see.\n\nEverything in \"What is already covered\" was measured on\n`entire-graph` main @ `3f632e4` and is a fact. Everything in \"Leads\" is a\n**lead**: a pattern that produced a real defect in brain, present in graph,\n**not** verified to be a defect here. Do not report a lead as a finding.\n\n---\n\n## Prerequisite that is itself a finding\n\n**Graph needs a C compiler on Windows. brain does not.**\n\nThe tree-sitter grammars are cgo — `internal/sem/grammars/*/binding.go` all\n`import \"C\"` — and there is no pure-Go fallback:\n\n```\n$ GOOS=windows CGO_ENABLED=0 go build ./...\nimports .../internal/sem/grammars/clojure: build constraints exclude all Go files\nimports .../internal/sem/grammars/csharp:  build constraints exclude all Go files\n... (every grammar, then the binary fails)\n```\n\nGitHub's `windows-latest` runners ship mingw-w64, so CI never meets this. A\ndeveloper's Windows machine usually does not. `verify-graph.ps1` section G0\nchecks for `gcc` first and says so plainly, because every other section depends\non it.\n\nWorth asking in the report: **is a C toolchain an acceptable install\nrequirement for a Windows user of this product, or does the released binary\nhide it?** The released artifact is built in CI, so an end user is probably\nfine and only contributors are hit. Check, do not assume.\n\n---\n\n## What is already covered — do not re-litigate these\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| `_windows.go` / `_windows_test.go` files | **29** |\n| `GOOS == \"windows\"` branches in tests | **118** |\n| Windows CI | `windows-test-prepare` → sharded `windows-test-shards` + `windows-test-other` + `windows-test-vet`, aggregated by `windows-test`, which is a required check on `main` |\n| `go vet` on native Windows | **yes** — `windows-test-vet` runs `go vet ./...` on `windows-latest` |\n| Symlink privilege | handled in several places already, including an explicit `syscall.Errno(1314)` (`ERROR_PRIVILEGE_NOT_HELD`) check in `provider_gitdir_unc_windows_test.go` |\n| Committed CRLF files | **0** |\n\nThis is a sharded full suite, not the compile-and-link gate wearing a test\nsuite's name that `cli` has. Graph is roughly where brain wants to be.\n\n---\n\n## The two CI legs that deliberately exclude Windows\n\nThese are stated in graph's own workflow, in its own words, so they are\nintentional rather than oversights. They are also the places where a Windows\ndefect could live indefinitely without anything going red.\n\n1. **`statusline`** — `matrix: os: [ubuntu-latest, macos-latest]`, running\n   `sh scripts/entire-graph-statusline_test.sh`. The shell script is a legacy\n   compatibility shim; what setup actually installs is\n   `StatusLineCommand = \"entire graph statusline\"`, which the source says is\n   \"the same on every OS\". So the shipped status line is the Go binary and is\n   probably fine — **but nothing proves it renders on a Windows terminal.**\n   G5 looks at this.\n2. **`prompt-context-hook`** — ubuntu and macOS only, with the reason written\n   in the workflow: *\"No windows-latest: the tests build fake binaries as\n   /bin/sh scripts and use `kill -9`.\"* That is an honest statement about the\n   **test harness**, not about the hook. The hook's behaviour on Windows is\n   therefore untested, in either direction. If you can run the hook by hand on\n   Windows, that is new information nobody has.\n\n`lint` (gofmt + `go vet`) is ubuntu-only, but `windows-test-vet` covers vet\nnatively and gofmt is OS-independent, so this one is not a gap.\n\n---\n\n## No `.gitattributes`\n\nGraph has none. brain has a good one. Today this costs nothing — **0 committed\nfiles have CRLF line endings** — so it is an unpinned invariant rather than a\nbug: nothing stops the next golden fixture or embedded asset from being\nnormalised by `core.autocrlf`, which **Git for Windows turns on by default**.\n\nIf you add one, port brain's shape: targeted rules naming embedded assets and\ngolden fixtures, not a blanket `* text=auto eol=lf`. Check first whether graph\nhas byte-exact fixtures that a checkout could mangle — if it does not, this is\na cheap guard; if it does, it is a real one.\n\n---\n\n## Leads — patterns that were real defects in brain\n\nEach of these is a grep you can run, not a claim. The brain column says what\nthe same pattern actually did there.\n\n### L1 — separator normalisation followed by `path/filepath`\n\n**In brain:** `pathIsUnder` normalised `\\` to `/` and then called\n`filepath.Clean`, which on Windows puts the backslashes straight back. The\nfollowing `HasPrefix(p, root+\"/\")` could never match, so the OneDrive warning\nwas dead on the only OS it was for. Six tests passed on macOS, where `Clean`\nleaves `/` alone. **The subject was OS-dependent and the tests were not.**\n\n```powershell\ncd work\\graph\ngit grep -n \"ReplaceAll\" -- \"*.go\" | Select-String '\\\\\\\\'\n```\n\nFiles that both rewrite separators and call `filepath.Clean`:\n`internal/sem/provider.go`, `internal/cli/stats.go`,\n`internal/agentsetup/files.go`. Graph mostly normalises **towards** backslash\n(`internal/agentsetup/files.go:1178`, `:1254`, `:1284`), which is the Windows\ndirection and the opposite of brain's mistake — so this may well be fine. What\nto check is whether any comparison built that way is then matched against a\nvalue normalised the other way.\n\nWhile you are in `files.go`: `pathsEqual` at `:492` and the ancestor check at\n`:496` compare `filepath.Clean(a) == filepath.Clean(b)` with **no case\nfolding**. On NTFS `C:\\Users\\X` and `C:\\users\\x` are the same directory and\nthat comparison says they are not. Whether any caller can reach it with\ndifferently-cased inputs is the question — `%LOCALAPPDATA%` versus a\nuser-typed path is the usual way it happens.\n\n### L2 — `os.Executable()` re-exec\n\n**In brain:** the detached worker re-exec'd `os.Executable()`, which under\n`go test` is the test binary. It read `memory worker --once` as positional\narguments and re-ran the whole package, reaching the launcher again — 34\nchained processes on Windows and a timed-out suite.\n\n```powershell\ngit grep -n \"os.Executable()\" -- \"*.go\"\n```\n\nThree sites: `cmd/graph-bench/main.go:132`,\n`internal/gitutil/process_job_windows.go:202`,\n`internal/cli/stats_cache.go:119`. The `process_job_windows.go` one is in\nWindows-only code and is the one worth reading. The guard brain uses is in\n`work\\brain\\internal\\cli\\memory_cmd.go` — `underGoTest()`, which checks the\nexecutable's name and `flag.Lookup(\"test.v\")`, deliberately not\n`testing.Testing()`, which would pull `testing` into the shipped binary.\n\n**If you see a process count climbing during G3, kill the run and report it\nimmediately.** That is this class, and it eats the machine.\n\n### L3 — symlinks without the privilege\n\n**In brain:** 32 tests failed instead of skipping on a machine where the\naccount lacks `SeCreateSymbolicLinkPrivilege` (the default without Developer\nMode).\n\nGraph has **156 `os.Symlink` call sites across 48 test files** and handles the\nprivilege in at least four places already. G4 is the measurement: how many of\nthe rest fail rather than skip. Note the distinction brain settled on — **not**\na blanket skip on Windows, because Developer Mode and CI *do* grant the\nprivilege and a blanket skip would hide real failures. Probe, then skip.\n\n### L4 — writing into the user's global agent settings\n\n**In brain:** when the repository root *is* the home directory,\n`<repo>/.claude/settings.json` is Claude Code's global settings file, and setup\ninstalled a `PreToolUse` hook into it that then fired in every session on the\nmachine.\n\nGraph writes to `.claude/settings.local.json` and\n`.claude/settings.json`, and `internal/cli/root.go:41` already carries a\n`homeDir` seam described as *\"the home whose .claude/settings.json setup...\"*,\nso some of this thinking has already happened here. Read\n`internal/agentsetup/claude_hook.go` and `claude_statusline.go` and check\nwhether the repo-root-equals-home case is refused. The brain guard is\n`isUserHomeDir` in `work\\brain\\internal\\cli\\agent_wiring.go`.\n\n---\n\n## What no CI runner can see\n\nThis is the real reason you are running graph on a physical machine.\n\n- **A OneDrive-redirected `%USERPROFILE%`.** Extremely common on managed\n  Windows machines, and sqlite WAL on a synced directory is unsafe. Runners\n  never have it.\n- **Developer Mode off / a non-administrator account.** Runners are\n  administrators.\n- **A real console.** TTY detection, colour, the status line, anything that\n  gates on `Caps.TTY`. Judging a TUI through a pipe is how you get this wrong —\n  run it in a real terminal window.\n- **The installed-plugin path**, `%LOCALAPPDATA%\\entire\\plugins\\...`, as\n  opposed to a repository checkout. This is where brain's worst defect lived.\n- **A real clone's path length**, against a real user's home directory rather\n  than `D:\\a\\repo\\repo`.\n- **Another process holding a file open** at the moment of an atomic replace.\n  Nothing in either repo reproduces a `SHARING_VIOLATION`, and it remains\n  unverified in both. If you manage to reproduce one, that is the most valuable\n  thing you could bring back.\nEOF\necho \"GRAPH-NOTES.md $(wc -l < \"$K3/GRAPH-NOTES.md\") lines\"","description":"Write the graph Windows notes"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1806,"cache_read_input_tokens":195347,"output_tokens":3915,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":189},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1806,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3915,"cache_read_input_tokens":195347,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1806,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1806},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"toolInputStreamMs":52888,"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01FNvj1W7e1fZF1yK9dWbdQi":{"command":"K3=/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-suhaan-devenv/93ae8948-c612-4072-8e70-2750e80df9ce/scratchpad/winkit3\ncat > \"$K3/GRAPH-NOTES.md\" <<'EOF'\n# entire-graph on Windows — the map\n\nRead this before `verify-graph.ps1`. Graph is in a **much better** Windows\nposition than brain was, and the useful work is therefore different: there is\nalmost nothing here about whether it compiles, and a lot about what a CI runner\nstructurally cannot see.\n\nEverything in \"What is already covered\" was measured on\n`entire-graph` main @ `3f632e4` and is a fact. Everything in \"Leads\" is a\n**lead**: a pattern that produced a real defect in brain, present in graph,\n**not** verified to be a defect here. Do not report a lead as a finding.\n\n---\n\n## Prerequisite that is itself a finding\n\n**Graph needs a C compiler on Windows. brain does not.**\n\nThe tree-sitter grammars are cgo — `internal/sem/grammars/*/binding.go` all\n`import \"C\"` — and there is no pure-Go fallback:\n\n```\n$ GOOS=windows CGO_ENABLED=0 go build ./...\nimports ..