{"parentUuid":"0406cc88-14e2-4a3f-b943-dfcc25892a0b","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"3519c29c-c027-4927-870b-4ea210ffb374","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017vnw6iCE7HWQwVDjBorgtW","type":"tool_result","content":"===== graph #416 =====\n{\"base\":\"main\",\"body\":\"\\u003e **From a Windows verification run. Needs the entire-graph owner's review before anything else happens to it.** One of four small draft PRs, one per defect class, from running graph's suite on a real Windows machine.\\n\\n## What this is\\n\\nFound by running the suite on Windows 11 Pro 10.0.26200 (amd64), `go1.27.4 windows/amd64`, gcc 16.2.0 (MinGW-w64 UCRT), not administrator, Developer Mode off, profile not OneDrive-redirected. The user profile `C:\\\\Users\\\\\\u003cuser\\u003e` is itself a git repository, so `%TEMP%` sits inside one. That configuration is common for people who keep dotfiles in git, and no CI runner has it.\\n\\n## The defect\\n\\n`agentsetup.Context` and `agentsetup.OuterRepo` find repositories by walking up for `.git` without invoking git, and neither honoured `GIT_CEILING_DIRECTORIES`. Git does, so they disagreed with git exactly where the variable is set: to keep an enclosing repository out of reach.\\n\\n- `Context` answered a directory git reports as outside any repository with the home folder.\\n- `OuterRepo` named the home folder as the \\\"outer repository\\\" of every top-level clone. Its one caller already ignores a home-folder result, so the user-visible effect is limited to the guide's destination. The tests, however, cannot be fenced at all.\\n\\nBoth walks now stop before stepping up into a ceiling, following git's rule. The starting directory is still examined, and empty and relative entries are ignored.\\n\\nThe comparison is **lexical**: cleaned, and case-folded on Windows. It never resolves an entry on disk, for the reason `resolveRepo` already states: a ceiling entry is caller-controlled, and resolving one can reach a UNC path and perform network I/O. The trade-off is that an entry spelled differently from the walk (an 8.3 short name against the long one) does not match, which leaves the walk exactly as unfenced as before. I chose this over importing `sem`'s ceiling resolver into `agentsetup`, which today imports no internal package. If you'd rather share that resolver, this is the place to say so.\\n\\n**Reproduce, on unpatched `main`** (temp dir inside the home-folder repository):\\n\\n```\\n--- FAIL: TestCoordinationOutsideRepository (0.00s)\\n    coordination_test.go:171: C:\\\\Users\\\\STHEMI~1 \\u003cnil\\u003e\\n--- FAIL: TestOuterRepoIsEmptyForATopLevelRepo (0.00s)\\n    nested_test.go:38: a top-level repository must report no outer repo, got \\\"C:\\\\\\\\Users\\\\\\\\STHEMI~1\\\"\\n--- FAIL: TestOuterRepoRequiresAnInnerRepository (0.04s)\\n    nested_test.go:112: a top-level repository has no outer repository, got \\\"C:\\\\\\\\Users\\\\\\\\STHEMI~1\\\"\\n```\\n\\n**After:**\\n\\n```\\n--- PASS: TestCoordinationOutsideRepository (0.00s)\\n--- PASS: TestContextHonoursGitCeilingDirectories (0.47s)\\n    --- PASS: .../no_ceiling_finds_the_enclosing_repository\\n    --- PASS: .../a_ceiling_at_the_repository_stops_the_walk\\n    --- PASS: .../a_ceiling_below_the_repository_stops_the_walk\\n    --- PASS: .../an_unrelated_ceiling_changes_nothing\\n    --- PASS: .../the_starting_directory_is_still_examined\\n    --- PASS: .../one_entry_of_a_list_is_enough\\n    --- PASS: .../letter_case_does_not_matter_where_the_filesystem_ignores_it\\n--- PASS: TestOuterRepoFindsTheProjectAroundANestedClone (0.00s)\\n--- PASS: TestOuterRepoIsEmptyForATopLevelRepo (0.00s)\\n--- PASS: TestOuterRepoDetectsAGitFileNotJustADirectory (0.00s)\\n--- PASS: TestOuterRepoRequiresAnInnerRepository (0.01s)\\n--- PASS: TestOuterRepoHonoursGitCeilingDirectories (0.01s)\\nok  \\tgithub.com/entireio/entire-graph/internal/agentsetup\\t1.066s\\n```\\n\\n`TestContextHonoursGitCeilingDirectories` also runs `git rev-parse --show-toplevel` for every case with the same environment and working directory, and git agreed with all seven.\\n\\n**The tests that fail without the fix.** `TestContextHonoursGitCeilingDirectories` and `TestOuterRepoHonoursGitCeilingDirectories` fail on any machine, enclosing repository or not. Two weakenings, identifiers left compiling:\\n\\n`isGitCeiling` always returns false:\\n\\n```\\n--- FAIL: TestContextHonoursGitCeilingDirectories (0.26s)\\n    --- FAIL: .../a_ceiling_at_the_repository_stops_the_walk (0.00s)\\n        coordination_test.go:235: Context from ...\\\\001\\\\inner\\\\deep with GIT_CEILING_DIRECTORIES=\\\"...\\\\\\\\001\\\" = \\\"...\\\\\\\\001\\\"; want a repository: false\\n    --- FAIL: .../a_ceiling_below_the_repository_stops_the_walk (0.00s)\\n    --- FAIL: .../one_entry_of_a_list_is_enough (0.00s)\\n    --- FAIL: .../letter_case_does_not_matter_where_the_filesystem_ignores_it (0.00s)\\n--- FAIL: TestOuterRepoHonoursGitCeilingDirectories (0.01s)\\n    nested_test.go:137: a ceiling at the outer repository must hide it: got \\\"...\\\\\\\\TestOuterRepoHonoursGitCeilingDirectories318842956\\\\\\\\001\\\"\\n```\\n\\nNo case folding:\\n\\n```\\n--- FAIL: TestContextHonoursGitCeilingDirectories (0.42s)\\n    --- FAIL: .../letter_case_does_not_matter_where_the_filesystem_ignores_it (0.00s)\\n```\\n\\nOne test-shape change to flag: `TestOuterRepoRequiresAnInnerRepository` is no longer `t.Parallel()`, because its last case needs `t.Setenv`, which a parallel test cannot use.\\n\\n## Verified\\n\\n- [x] `go test ./internal/agentsetup` on Windows, on this branch and on a combination with the other three PRs, in a fresh clone with `%TEMP%` outside any repository\\n- [x] same package, with `%TEMP%` inside the home-folder repository: the three tests above now pass there too\\n- [x] `go build ./...` and `go vet ./...` on Windows with cgo\\n- [x] `gofmt -l -s` empty on an LF checkout (a default Windows checkout with `core.autocrlf=true` lists every file, because graph has no `.gitattributes`; that is separate)\\n\\n## NOT verified\\n\\n- Behaviour with a UNC or 8.3-spelled ceiling entry beyond \\\"does not match, walk unchanged\\\"; no network share was available.\\n- macOS and Linux were not run locally. The change has no OS-specific code path except the Windows case fold, so CI is the check.\\n\",\"commits\":[\"fix(agentsetup): honour GIT_CEILING_DIRECTORIES in the repository walks\"],\"title\":\"fix(agentsetup): honour GIT_CEILING_DIRECTORIES in the repository walks\"}\n\n===== graph #417 =====\n{\"base\":\"main\",\"body\":\"\\u003e **From a Windows verification run. Needs the entire-graph owner's review before anything else happens to it.** One of four small draft PRs, one per defect class, from running graph's suite on a real Windows machine.\\n\\n## What this is\\n\\nFound by running the suite on Windows 11 Pro 10.0.26200 (amd64), `go1.27.4`, gcc 16.2.0, on a standard (non-administrator) account with Developer Mode **off**. That is the default Windows configuration, and in it `os.Symlink` is refused with `ERROR_PRIVILEGE_NOT_HELD`. CI runners are administrators, so they never see this.\\n\\n## The defect\\n\\nFour tests build a symlink fixture without first checking that the host can make one, so they **fail** instead of skipping:\\n\\n```\\n--- FAIL: TestActivationContainedAlias (0.00s)\\n    activation_test.go:103: symlink ..\\\\guide.md C:\\\\...\\\\.entire\\\\agent-guide.md: A required privilege is not held by the client.\\n--- FAIL: TestActivationDoesNotReadOutsideRepo (0.00s)\\n    activation_test.go:115: symlink C:\\\\...\\\\guide.md C:\\\\...\\\\.entire\\\\agent-guide.md: A required privilege is not held by the client.\\n--- FAIL: TestCoordinationLegacyAliasesAndProtectedTargets (0.01s)\\n    --- FAIL: .../non-markdown_generated_legacy_landing (0.00s)\\n    --- FAIL: .../outside (0.00s)\\n    --- FAIL: .../git (0.00s)\\n    --- FAIL: .../instruction-alias (0.00s)\\n--- FAIL: TestSelectiveCacheFailsClosedOnUnreadableGenerationMarker/escaping_symlink (2.99s)\\n    cache_generation_marker_test.go:113: symlink ...: A required privilege is not held by the client.\\n```\\n\\nEach now follows its own package's existing idiom:\\n\\n- **`agentsetup`**: call `skipIfSymlinksUnrepresentable` first. Its comment already states the contract: probe first, and `symlinkForTest` is fatal because a refusal after a successful probe is real.\\n- **`sem`**: skip on the `os.Symlink` error, as `TestCacheEntryReadRejectsSymlinkEscape` and the package's other symlink tests do.\\n\\nThis is deliberately **not** a blanket Windows skip. Where symlinks work (Developer Mode, elevated, CI), every one of these still runs.\\n\\n**After:**\\n\\n```\\n    activation_test.go:100: symlinks unavailable: symlink target ...\\\\link: A required privilege is not held by the client.\\n--- SKIP: TestActivationContainedAlias (0.00s)\\n--- SKIP: TestActivationDoesNotReadOutsideRepo (0.00s)\\n--- SKIP: TestCoordinationLegacyAliasesAndProtectedTargets (0.00s)\\n--- PASS: TestSymlinkFixturesProbeFirst (0.01s)\\n\\n--- PASS: TestSelectiveCacheFailsClosedOnUnreadableGenerationMarker (0.00s)\\n    --- SKIP: .../escaping_symlink (2.56s)\\n    --- PASS: .../not_a_gzip_stream (3.07s)\\n    --- PASS: .../directory_at_the_marker_name (3.07s)\\n```\\n\\n**The test that fails without it.** Nothing a pull request is checked on can see a missing probe, because CI grants the privilege. `TestSymlinkFixturesProbeFirst` (new, `agentsetup`) enforces the contract from source on every OS: any `Test` function whose first call to either helper is `symlinkForTest` fails, naming it. Without the three probes it fails listing exactly the three agentsetup tests, and no others:\\n\\n```\\n--- FAIL: TestSymlinkFixturesProbeFirst (0.01s)\\n    symlink_probe_guard_test.go:71: these tests build a symlink fixture before calling skipIfSymlinksUnrepresentable, so they FAIL instead of skipping on a host without the symlink privilege:\\n          activation_test.go: TestActivationContainedAlias\\n          activation_test.go: TestActivationDoesNotReadOutsideRepo\\n          coordination_test.go: TestCoordinationLegacyAliasesAndProtectedTargets\\n```\\n\\nThe guard covers `agentsetup` only. `sem` uses inline `os.Symlink` with no shared helper, so there is nothing equivalent to anchor a guard on there.\\n\\n## Verified\\n\\n- [x] `go test ./internal/agentsetup` and the affected `sem` test on an unprivileged Windows account, fresh clone, `%TEMP%` outside any repository\\n- [x] combined with the other three PRs: `agentsetup` green\\n- [x] `go vet ./...` on Windows; `gofmt -l -s` empty for the changed files\\n\\n## NOT verified\\n\\n- These tests' symlink paths were not exercised: this account has no symlink privilege, which is the point. CI runs them.\\n- `sem` has 156 `os.Symlink` call sites across 48 test files. Only the one that failed here was changed; the others either already skip or did not run into the refusal on this machine.\\n\",\"commits\":[\"test: skip symlink fixtures where the host withholds the privilege\"],\"title\":\"test: skip symlink fixtures where the host withholds the privilege\"}\n\n===== graph #418 =====\n{\"base\":\"main\",\"body\":\"\\u003e **From a Windows verification run. Needs the entire-graph owner's review before anything else happens to it.** One of four small draft PRs, one per defect class, from running graph's suite on a real Windows machine.\\n\\n## What this is\\n\\nFound by running the suite on Windows 11 Pro 10.0.26200 (amd64), `go1.27.4`, gcc 16.2.0, with Git for Windows 2.56 installed the default way.\\n\\n## The defect\\n\\n`TestSearchRepositoryVerifyFromASubdirectoryRunsAgainstTheRealModule` replays the emitted verify command through `sh -c`. It already skips when the `go` toolchain is missing, but not when `sh` is. Git for Windows ships `sh` but does not put it on `PATH` by default; the Windows CI runners do. So on a stock developer machine:\\n\\n```\\n--- FAIL: TestSearchRepositoryVerifyFromASubdirectoryRunsAgainstTheRealModule (2.33s)\\n    search_verify_scope_test.go:346: emitted command failed from the scope root: exec: \\\"sh\\\": executable file not found in %PATH%\\n```\\n\\nThe test now skips the replay when `sh` is not on `PATH`, in the same way it already does for `go`. The derivation assertions above the replay still run everywhere.\\n\\n**After, without `sh` on PATH:**\\n\\n```\\n    search_verify_scope_test.go:344: sh not on PATH; the derivation is pinned above\\n--- SKIP: TestSearchRepositoryVerifyFromASubdirectoryRunsAgainstTheRealModule (2.38s)\\n```\\n\\n**After, with Git's `sh` put on PATH**, so the replay really runs on Windows:\\n\\n```\\n--- PASS: TestSearchRepositoryVerifyFromASubdirectoryRunsAgainstTheRealModule (3.35s)\\n```\\n\\nThis is a test-only change. The \\\"fails without it\\\" evidence is the unpatched output above, on a machine without `sh` on `PATH`.\\n\\n## A related lead, not addressed here\\n\\nThe emitted verify command is POSIX-shell syntax (`go test ./cmd/tool/ledger -run '^TestReconcileLedger$'`). It runs as-is in PowerShell, but in `cmd.exe` single quotes are literal, so the `-run` pattern would carry the quotes. I did not test `cmd.exe` and have not made this a finding.\\n\\n## Verified\\n\\n- [x] the test on Windows, with and without `sh` on `PATH`\\n- [x] `go vet ./internal/sem`; `gofmt -l -s` empty for the changed file\\n\\n## NOT verified\\n\\n- Nothing else changed. CI already has `sh`, so CI will run the replay as before.\\n\",\"commits\":[\"test(sem): skip the verify-command replay when sh is not on PATH\"],\"title\":\"test(sem): skip the verify-command replay when sh is not on PATH\"}\n\n===== graph #419 =====\n{\"base\":\"main\",\"body\":\"\\u003e **From a Windows verification run. Needs the entire-graph owner's review before anything else happens to it.** One of four small draft PRs, one per defect class, from running graph's suite on a real Windows machine.\\n\\n## What this is\\n\\nFound while exercising the CLI end to end on Windows 11 Pro 10.0.26200 (amd64), in Windows PowerShell 5.1, which is the shell a stock Windows machine has.\\n\\n## The defect\\n\\nThe documented flow is to write a compact snapshot to a file and query it:\\n\\n```powershell\\nentire graph snapshot --repo . --format compact-ndjson \\u003e graph.compact.ndjson\\nentire graph snapshot-query --input graph.compact.ndjson --symbol ProcessAlive --format ndjson\\n```\\n\\nIn Windows PowerShell 5.1, `Out-File -Encoding utf8` and `Set-Content -Encoding utf8` write a UTF-8 byte-order mark, and so does `\\u003e` wherever the profile makes UTF-8 the default. `snapshot-query` then refuses the file on its first byte:\\n\\n```\\nload compact snapshot: compact snapshot line 1: invalid character '﻿' looking for beginning of value\\n```\\n\\nThe same snapshot written through `cmd.exe` (raw bytes) loads and queries fine. Measured on one machine, same binary:\\n\\n| How the file was written | First bytes | `snapshot-query` |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| `cmd /c ... \\u003e file` | `91,34` (`[\\\"`) | exit 0, symbol found |\\n| PowerShell 5.1 `\\u003e` on this machine | `239,187,191` | exit 1, error above |\\n| `Set-Content -Encoding utf8` | `239,187,191` | exit 1, error above |\\n\\n`DecodeCompactSnapshot` now skips **one** leading BOM. That is the rule this package already applies to git config (`TestGitMetadataGuardAllowsUTF8BOMAtConfigStart` / `...RejectsMisplacedUTF8BOM`) and `agentsetup` applies to Claude settings (\\\"Windows PowerShell 5.1 writes one\\\"). A second BOM, or one at the start of any later line, is still refused.\\n\\n**After**, a binary built from this branch against the file PowerShell wrote:\\n\\n```\\n{\\\"record_type\\\":\\\"symbol\\\",\\\"id\\\":\\\"local/repo:Go:internal/cli/internal/brainstore/process_alive_windows.go:function:ProcessAlive\\\", ...}\\nexit 0\\n```\\n\\n**The tests.** `TestLoadCompactSnapshotAllowsUTF8BOMAtStart` checks that a BOM-prefixed snapshot loads, decodes to the same canonical semantic hash, and answers the same query. `TestLoadCompactSnapshotRejectsMisplacedUTF8BOM` pins that a doubled BOM and a BOM on line 2 are still refused, and that the error names the line.\\n\\nWith the BOM check disabled (identifiers left compiling):\\n\\n```\\n--- FAIL: TestLoadCompactSnapshotAllowsUTF8BOMAtStart (0.00s)\\n    compact_snapshot_bom_test.go:29: a snapshot saved by Windows PowerShell 5.1 (leading UTF-8 BOM) was refused: compact snapshot line 1: invalid character '﻿' looking for beginning of value\\n```\\n\\nAll 47 existing compact-snapshot tests still pass.\\n\\n## Verified\\n\\n- [x] the new tests plus `-run 'CompactSnapshot|Compact'` in `internal/sem` on Windows\\n- [x] end to end: `snapshot` → PowerShell-written file → `snapshot-query`, with a binary from this branch\\n- [x] `go vet ./internal/sem`; `gofmt -l -s` empty for the changed files\\n\\n## NOT verified / not addressed\\n\\n- **Stock Windows PowerShell 5.1 `\\u003e` writes UTF-16LE**, not UTF-8 with a BOM. This change does not decode UTF-16. That is a different encoding rather than a save artifact, and accepting it is a larger decision. A `--output \\u003cfile\\u003e` flag on `snapshot` would sidestep shell encodings entirely; that is your call.\\n- Only `snapshot-query`'s loader was changed. Other `--input`-style readers were not audited for the same issue.\\n\",\"commits\":[\"fix(sem): accept a leading UTF-8 BOM in a compact snapshot\"],\"title\":\"fix(sem): accept a leading UTF-8 BOM in a compact snapshot\"}","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"f2c7e097-25c8-459e-b16d-9531a687a5a7","timestamp":"2026-11-19T01:17:55.562Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"===== graph #416 =====\n{\"base\":\"main\",\"body\":\"\\u003e **From a Windows verification run. Needs the entire-graph owner's review before anything else happens to it.** One of four small draft PRs, one per defect class, from running graph's suite on a real Windows machine.\\n\\n## What this is\\n\\nFound by running the suite on Windows 11 Pro 10.0.26200 (amd64), `go1.27.4 windows/amd64`, gcc 16.2.0 (MinGW-w64 UCRT), not administrator, Developer Mode off, profile not OneDrive-redirected. The user profile `C:\\\\Users\\\\\\u003cuser\\u003e` is itself a git repository, so `%TEMP%` sits inside one. That configuration is common for people who keep dotfiles in git, and no CI runner has it.\\n\\n## The defect\\n\\n`agentsetup.Context` and `agentsetup.OuterRepo` find repositories by walking up for `.git` without invoking git, and neither honoured `GIT_CEILING_DIRECTORIES`. Git does, so they disagreed with git exactly where the variable is set: to keep an enclosing repository out of reach.\\n\\n- `Context` answered a directory git reports as outside any repository with the home folder.\\n- `OuterRepo` named the home folder as the \\\"outer repository\\\" of every top-level clone. Its one caller already ignores a home-folder result, so the user-visible effect is limited to the guide's destination. The tests, however, cannot be fenced at all.\\n\\nBoth walks now stop before stepping up into a ceiling, following git's rule. The starting directory is still examined, and empty and relative entries are ignored.\\n\\nThe comparison is **lexical**: cleaned, and case-folded on Windows. It never resolves an entry on disk, for the reason `resolveRepo` already states: a ceiling entry is caller-controlled, and resolving one can reach a UNC path and perform network I/O. The trade-off is that an entry spelled differently from the walk (an 8.3 short name against the long one) does not match, which leaves the walk exactly as unfenced as before. I chose this over importing `sem`'s ceiling resolver into `agentsetup`, which today imports no internal package. If you'd rather share that resolver, this is the place to say so.\\n\\n**Reproduce, on unpatched `main`** (temp dir inside the home-folder repository):\\n\\n```\\n--- FAIL: TestCoordinationOutsideRepository (0.00s)\\n    coordination_test.go:171: C:\\\\Users\\\\STHEMI~1 \\u003cnil\\u003e\\n--- FAIL: TestOuterRepoIsEmptyForATopLevelRepo (0.00s)\\n    nested_test.go:38: a top-level repository must report no outer repo, got \\\"C:\\\\\\\\Users\\\\\\\\STHEMI~1\\\"\\n--- FAIL: TestOuterRepoRequiresAnInnerRepository (0.04s)\\n    nested_test.go:112: a top-level repository has no outer repository, got \\\"C:\\\\\\\\Users\\\\\\\\STHEMI~1\\\"\\n```\\n\\n**After:**\\n\\n```\\n--- PASS: TestCoordinationOutsideRepository (0.00s)\\n--- PASS: TestContextHonoursGitCeilingDirectories (0.47s)\\n    --- PASS: .../no_ceiling_finds_the_enclosing_repository\\n    --- PASS: .../a_ceiling_at_the_repository_stops_the_walk\\n    --- PASS: .../a_ceiling_below_the_repository_stops_the_walk\\n    --- PASS: .../an_unrelated_ceiling_changes_nothing\\n    --- PASS: .../the_starting_directory_is_still_examined\\n    --- PASS: .../one_entry_of_a_list_is_enough\\n    --- PASS: .../letter_case_does_not_matter_where_the_filesystem_ignores_it\\n--- PASS: TestOuterRepoFindsTheProjectAroundANestedClone (0.00s)\\n--- PASS: TestOuterRepoIsEmptyForATopLevelRepo (0.00s)\\n--- PASS: TestOuterRepoDetectsAGitFileNotJustADirectory (0.00s)\\n--- PASS: TestOuterRepoRequiresAnInnerRepository (0.01s)\\n--- PASS: TestOuterRepoHonoursGitCeilingDirectories (0.01s)\\nok  \\tgithub.com/entireio/entire-graph/internal/agentsetup\\t1.066s\\n```\\n\\n`TestContextHonoursGitCeilingDirectories` also runs `git rev-parse --show-toplevel` for every case with the same environment and working directory, and git agreed with all seven.\\n\\n**The tests that fail without the fix.** `TestContextHonoursGitCeilingDirectories` and `TestOuterRepoHonoursGitCeilingDirectories` fail on any machine, enclosing repository or not. Two weakenings, identifiers left compiling:\\n\\n`isGitCeiling` always returns false:\\n\\n```\\n--- FAIL: TestContextHonoursGitCeilingDirectories (0.26s)\\n    --- FAIL: .../a_ceiling_at_the_repository_stops_the_walk (0.00s)\\n        coordination_test.go:235: Context from ...\\\\001\\\\inner\\\\deep with GIT_CEILING_DIRECTORIES=\\\"...\\\\\\\\001\\\" = \\\"...\\\\\\\\001\\\"; want a repository: false\\n    --- FAIL: .../a_ceiling_below_the_repository_stops_the_walk (0.00s)\\n    --- FAIL: .../one_entry_of_a_list_is_enough (0.00s)\\n    --- FAIL: .../letter_case_does_not_matter_where_the_filesystem_ignores_it (0.00s)\\n--- FAIL: TestOuterRepoHonoursGitCeilingDirectories (0.01s)\\n    nested_test.go:137: a ceiling at the outer repository must hide it: got \\\"...\\\\\\\\TestOuterRepoHonoursGitCeilingDirectories318842956\\\\\\\\001\\\"\\n```\\n\\nNo case folding:\\n\\n```\\n--- FAIL: TestContextHonoursGitCeilingDirectories (0.42s)\\n    --- FAIL: .../letter_case_does_not_matter_where_the_filesystem_ignores_it (0.00s)\\n```\\n\\nOne test-shape change to flag: `TestOuterRepoRequiresAnInnerRepository` is no longer `t.Parallel()`, because its last case needs `t.Setenv`, which a parallel test cannot use.\\n\\n## Verified\\n\\n- [x] `go test ./internal/agentsetup` on Windows, on this branch and on a combination with the other three PRs, in a fresh clone with `%TEMP%` outside any repository\\n- [x] same package, with `%TEMP%` inside the home-folder repository: the three tests above now pass there too\\n- [x] `go build ./...` and `go vet ./...` on Windows with cgo\\n- [x] `gofmt -l -s` empty on an LF checkout (a default Windows checkout with `core.autocrlf=true` lists every file, because graph has no `.gitattributes`; that is separate)\\n\\n## NOT verified\\n\\n- Behaviour with a UNC or 8.3-spelled ceiling entry beyond \\\"does not match, walk unchanged\\\"; no network share was available.\\n- macOS and Linux were not run locally. The change has no OS-specific code path except the Windows case fold, so CI is the check.\\n\",\"commits\":[\"fix(agentsetup): honour GIT_CEILING_DIRECTORIES in the repository walks\"],\"title\":\"fix(agentsetup): honour GIT_CEILING_DIRECTORIES in the repository walks\"}\n\n===== graph #417 =====\n{\"base\":\"main\",\"body\":\"\\u003e **From a Windows verification run. Needs the entire-graph owner's review before anything else happens to it.** One of four small draft PRs, one per defect class, from running graph's suite on a real Windows machine.\\n\\n## What this is\\n\\nFound by running the suite on Windows 11 Pro 10.0.26200 (amd64), `go1.27.4`, gcc 16.2.0, on a standard (non-administrator) account with Developer Mode **off**. That is the default Windows configuration, and in it `os.Symlink` is refused with `ERROR_PRIVILEGE_NOT_HELD`. CI runners are administrators, so they never see this.\\n\\n## The defect\\n\\nFour tests build a symlink fixture without first checking that the host can make one, so they **fail** instead of skipping:\\n\\n```\\n--- FAIL: TestActivationContainedAlias (0.00s)\\n    activation_test.go:103: symlink ..\\\\guide.md C:\\\\...\\\\.entire\\\\agent-guide.md: A required privilege is not held by the client.\\n--- FAIL: TestActivationDoesNotReadOutsideRepo (0.00s)\\n    activation_test.go:115: symlink C:\\\\...\\\\guide.md C:\\\\...\\\\.entire\\\\agent-guide.md: A required privilege is not held by the client.\\n--- FAIL: TestCoordinationLegacyAliasesAndProtectedTargets (0.01s)\\n    --- FAIL: .../non-markdown_generated_legacy_landing (0.00s)\\n    --- FAIL: .../outside (0.00s)\\n    --- FAIL: .../git (0.00s)\\n    --- FAIL: .../instruction-alias (0.00s)\\n--- FAIL: TestSelectiveCacheFailsClosedOnUnreadableGenerationMarker/escaping_symlink (2.99s)\\n    cache_generation_marker_test.go:113: symlink ...: A required privilege is not held by the client.\\n```\\n\\nEach now follows its own package's existing idiom:\\n\\n- **`agentsetup`**: call `skipIfSymlinksUnrepresentable` first. Its comment already states the contract: probe first, and `symlinkForTest` is fatal because a refusal after a successful probe is real.\\n- **`sem`**: skip on the `os.Symlink` error, as `TestCacheEntryReadRejectsSymlinkEscape` and the package's other symlink tests do.\\n\\nThis is deliberately **not** a blanket Windows skip. Where symlinks work (Developer Mode, elevated, CI), every one of these still runs.\\n\\n**After:**\\n\\n```\\n    activation_test.go:100: symlinks unavailable: symlink target ...\\\\link: A required privilege is not held by the client.\\n--- SKIP: TestActivationContainedAlias (0.00s)\\n--- SKIP: TestActivationDoesNotReadOutsideRepo (0.00s)\\n--- SKIP: TestCoordinationLegacyAliasesAndProtectedTargets (0.00s)\\n--- PASS: TestSymlinkFixturesProbeFirst (0.01s)\\n\\n--- PASS: TestSelectiveCacheFailsClosedOnUnreadableGenerationMarker (0.00s)\\n    --- SKIP: .../escaping_symlink (2.56s)\\n    --- PASS: .../not_a_gzip_stream (3.07s)\\n    --- PASS: .../directory_at_the_marker_name (3.07s)\\n```\\n\\n**The test that fails without it.** Nothing a pull request is checked on can see a missing probe, because CI grants the privilege. `TestSymlinkFixturesProbeFirst` (new, `agentsetup`) enforces the contract from source on every OS: any `Test` function whose first call to either helper is `symlinkForTest` fails, naming it. Without the three probes it fails listing exactly the three agentsetup tests, and no others:\\n\\n```\\n--- FAIL: TestSymlinkFixturesProbeFirst (0.01s)\\n    symlink_probe_guard_test.go:71: these tests build a symlink fixture before calling skipIfSymlinksUnrepresentable, so they FAIL instead of skipping on a host without the symlink privilege:\\n          activation_test.go: TestActivationContainedAlias\\n          activation_test.go: TestActivationDoesNotReadOutsideRepo\\n          coordination_test.go: TestCoordinationLegacyAliasesAndProtectedTargets\\n```\\n\\nThe guard covers `agentsetup` only. `sem` uses inline `os.Symlink` with no shared helper, so there is nothing equivalent to anchor a guard on there.\\n\\n## Verified\\n\\n- [x] `go test ./internal/agentsetup` and the affected `sem` test on an unprivileged Windows account, fresh clone, `%TEMP%` outside any repository\\n- [x] combined with the other three PRs: `agentsetup` green\\n- [x] `go vet ./...` on Windows; `gofmt -l -s` empty for the changed files\\n\\n## NOT verified\\n\\n- These tests' symlink paths were not exercised: this account has no symlink privilege, which is the point. CI runs them.\\n- `sem` has 156 `os.Symlink` call sites across 48 test files. Only the one that failed here was changed; the others either already skip or did not run into the refusal on this machine.\\n\",\"commits\":[\"test: skip symlink fixtures where the host withholds the privilege\"],\"title\":\"test: skip symlink fixtures where the host withholds the privilege\"}\n\n===== graph #418 =====\n{\"base\":\"main\",\"body\":\"\\u003e **From a Windows verification run. Needs the entire-graph owner's review before anything else happens to it.** One of four small draft PRs, one per defect class, from running graph's suite on a real Windows machine.\\n\\n## What this is\\n\\nFound by running the suite on Windows 11 Pro 10.0.26200 (amd64), `go1.27.4`, gcc 16.2.0, with Git for Windows 2.56 installed the default way.\\n\\n## The defect\\n\\n`TestSearchRepositoryVerifyFromASubdirectoryRunsAgainstTheRealModule` replays the emitted verify command through `sh -c`. It already skips when the `go` toolchain is missing, but not when `sh` is. Git for Windows ships `sh` but does not put it on `PATH` by default; the Windows CI runners do. So on a stock developer machine:\\n\\n```\\n--- FAIL: TestSearchRepositoryVerifyFromASubdirectoryRunsAgainstTheRealModule (2.33s)\\n    search_verify_scope_test.go:346: emitted command failed from the scope root: exec: \\\"sh\\\": executable file not found in %PATH%\\n```\\n\\nThe test now skips the replay when `sh` is not on `PATH`, in the same way it already does for `go`. The derivation assertions above the replay still run everywhere.\\n\\n**After, without `sh` on PATH:**\\n\\n```\\n    search_verify_scope_test.go:344: sh not on PATH; the derivation is pinned above\\n--- SKIP: TestSearchRepositoryVerifyFromASubdirectoryRunsAgainstTheRealModule (2.38s)\\n```\\n\\n**After, with Git's `sh` put on PATH**, so the replay really runs on Windows:\\n\\n```\\n--- PASS: TestSearchRepositoryVerifyFromASubdirectoryRunsAgainstTheRealModule (3.35s)\\n```\\n\\nThis is a test-only change. The \\\"fails without it\\\" evidence is the unpatched output above, on a machine without `sh` on `PATH`.\\n\\n## A related lead, not addressed here\\n\\nThe emitted verify command is POSIX-shell syntax (`go test ./cmd/tool/ledger -run '^TestReconcileLedger$'`). It runs as-is in PowerShell, but in `cmd.exe` single quotes are literal, so the `-run` pattern would carry the quotes. I did not test `cmd.exe` and have not made this a finding.\\n\\n## Verified\\n\\n- [x] the test on Windows, with and without `sh` on `PATH`\\n- [x] `go vet ./internal/sem`; `gofmt -l -s` empty for the changed file\\n\\n## NOT verified\\n\\n- Nothing else changed. CI already has `sh`, so CI will run the replay as before.\\n\",\"commits\":[\"test(sem): skip the verify-command replay when sh is not on PATH\"],\"title\":\"test(sem): skip the verify-command replay when sh is not on PATH\"}\n\n===== graph #419 =====\n{\"base\":\"main\",\"body\":\"\\u003e **From a Windows verification run. Needs the entire-graph owner's review before anything else happens to it.** One of four small draft PRs, one per defect class, from running graph's suite on a real Windows machine.\\n\\n## What this is\\n\\nFound while exercising the CLI end to end on Windows 11 Pro 10.0.26200 (amd64), in Windows PowerShell 5.1, which is the shell a stock Windows machine has.\\n\\n## The defect\\n\\nThe documented flow is to write a compact snapshot to a file and query it:\\n\\n```powershell\\nentire graph snapshot --repo . --format compact-ndjson \\u003e graph.compact.ndjson\\nentire graph snapshot-query --input graph.compact.ndjson --symbol ProcessAlive --format ndjson\\n```\\n\\nIn Windows PowerShell 5.1, `Out-File -Encoding utf8` and `Set-Content -Encoding utf8` write a UTF-8 byte-order mark, and so does `\\u003e` wherever the profile makes UTF-8 the default. `snapshot-query` then refuses the file on its first byte:\\n\\n```\\nload compact snapshot: compact snapshot line 1: invalid character '﻿' looking for beginning of value\\n```\\n\\nThe same snapshot written through `cmd.exe` (raw bytes) loads and queries fine. Measured on one machine, same binary:\\n\\n| How the file was written | First bytes | `snapshot-query` |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| `cmd /c ... \\u003e file` | `91,34` (`[\\\"`) | exit 0, symbol found |\\n| PowerShell 5.1 `\\u003e` on this machine | `239,187,191` | exit 1, error above |\\n| `Set-Content -Encoding utf8` | `239,187,191` | exit 1, error above |\\n\\n`DecodeCompactSnapshot` now skips **one** leading BOM. That is the rule this package already applies to git config (`TestGitMetadataGuardAllowsUTF8BOMAtConfigStart` / `...RejectsMisplacedUTF8BOM`) and `agentsetup` applies to Claude settings (\\\"Windows PowerShell 5.1 writes one\\\"). A second BOM, or one at the start of any later line, is still refused.\\n\\n**After**, a binary built from this branch against the file PowerShell wrote:\\n\\n```\\n{\\\"record_type\\\":\\\"symbol\\\",\\\"id\\\":\\\"local/repo:Go:internal/cli/internal/brainstore/process_alive_windows.go:function:ProcessAlive\\\", ...}\\nexit 0\\n```\\n\\n**The tests.** `TestLoadCompactSnapshotAllowsUTF8BOMAtStart` checks that a BOM-prefixed snapshot loads, decodes to the same canonical semantic hash, and answers the same query. `TestLoadCompactSnapshotRejectsMisplacedUTF8BOM` pins that a doubled BOM and a BOM on line 2 are still refused, and that the error names the line.\\n\\nWith the BOM check disabled (identifiers left compiling):\\n\\n```\\n--- FAIL: TestLoadCompactSnapshotAllowsUTF8BOMAtStart (0.00s)\\n    compact_snapshot_bom_test.go:29: a snapshot saved by Windows PowerShell 5.1 (leading UTF-8 BOM) was refused: compact snapshot line 1: invalid character '﻿' looking for beginning of value\\n```\\n\\nAll 47 existing compact-snapshot tests still pass.\\n\\n## Verified\\n\\n- [x] the new tests plus `-run 'CompactSnapshot|Compact'` in `internal/sem` on Windows\\n- [x] end to end: `snapshot` → PowerShell-written file → `snapshot-query`, with a binary from this branch\\n- [x] `go vet ./internal/sem`; `gofmt -l -s` empty for the changed files\\n\\n## NOT verified / not addressed\\n\\n- **Stock Windows PowerShell 5.1 `\\u003e` writes UTF-16LE**, not UTF-8 with a BOM. This change does not decode UTF-16. That is a different encoding rather than a save artifact, and accepting it is a larger decision. A `--output \\u003cfile\\u003e` flag on `snapshot` would sidestep shell encodings entirely; that is your call.\\n- Only `snapshot-query`'s loader was changed. Other `--input`-style readers were not audited for the same issue.\\n\",\"commits\":[\"fix(sem): accept a leading UTF-8 BOM in a compact snapshot\"],\"title\":\"fix(sem): accept a leading UTF-8 BOM in a compact snapshot\"}","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"99dda994-167a-4e8a-ab55-30c3cf8d9ea8","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv","root":"/Users/suhaan/devenv","branch":"main","default_branch":null,"status":{"clean":false,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":1,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":273},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remote":"entirehq/devenv","visibility":"private"},"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[{"name":"origin","host":"github.com","remote":"entirehq/devenv","visibility":"private"}],"visibility_cache":[{"host":"github.com","remote":"entirehq/devenv","visibility":"private"},{"host":"github.com","remote":"entireio/entire-brain","visibility":"public"},{"host":"github.com","remote":"entireio/entire-graph","visibility":"public"}]}},"live_cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv","platform":"macos"}},"sandboxed":false,"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"0406cc88-14e2-4a3f-b943-dfcc25892a0b","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"session_id":"1041b1a0-c015-41c2-a29f-4f2db2f6e0cb","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv","sessionId":"93ae8948-c612-4072-8e70-2750e80df9ce","version":"2.1.323","gitBranch":"main","slug":"floofy-discovering-bachman"}
