the same environment and cwd. Differential testing","+against git itself is the strongest form this check could take. Staying lexical rather","+than resolving ceiling entries fails in the safe direction: a mismatch leaves the walk as","+unfenced as before, never more fenced.","+","+### The one I would not merge as a closing fix: #419","+","+Windows PowerShell 5.1's `>` defaults to **UTF-16LE**, not UTF-8-with-BOM. The PR's own","+table shows a UTF-8 BOM only because that machine's profile had changed the default, and","+its \"NOT verified\" section says as much. So on a stock Windows box the documented flow","+","+    entire graph snapshot --repo . --format compact-ndjson > graph.compact.ndjson","+    entire graph snapshot-query --input graph.compact.ndjson --symbol Foo","+","+**still fails**, with a different error. The BOM skip is correct and worth having on its","+own terms — it matches what the git-config guard and `agentsetup`'s Claude-settings reader","+already do — but the change that actually closes the flow is the `--output <file>` flag the","+PR proposes and leaves to you. Worth taking the fix; not worth closing the issue on it.","+","+### Decisions that are yours, not the agent's","+","+1. **Shared ceiling resolver, or two copies?** #416 adds `internal/agentsetup/gitceiling.go`","+   rather than importing `sem`'s resolver, because `agentsetup` today imports no internal","+   package. brain landed the same logic inline in `internal/agentsetup/migration.go` in its","+   own PR #654. That is now **three** copies of git's ceiling rule across two repos, already","+   diverging in shape. You and brain already share the guide renderer (graph #366 / brain","+   #447), so there is precedent for sharing this too. The PR explicitly invites you to say so.","+2. **graph has no `.gitattributes` at all.** With `core.autocrlf=true` (the Git for Windows","+   default) a stock Windows clone checks out every `.go` file with CRLF, and `gofmt -l -s .`","+   then lists all of them — so graph's gofmt gate cannot pass on a default Windows clone.","+   Every golden fixture is unpinned for the same reason. brain hit this too; the brain-lane","+   fix is `*.go text eol=lf` plus a class guard (branch `win/go-eol-lf`), verified with","+   `git add --renormalize .` rewriting zero files. The same two lines would do it here, and","+   it is cheap. Not my lane, so I have not written it for you.","+3. **`sem` symlink coverage.** #417 fixes the one `sem` site that failed and adds an","+   AST-based class guard, but the guard covers `agentsetup` only; `sem` has 156 `os.Symlink`","+   call sites across 48 test files with no shared helper to anchor a guard on. Whether that","+   is worth a helper is your call.","+4. **cgo stays a Windows prerequisite.** The tree-sitter grammars all `import \"C\"` with no","+   pure-Go fallback, so `GOOS=windows CGO_ENABLED=0 go build ./...` fails for every grammar.","+   Irrelevant for released binaries; a real barrier for a Windows contributor, who needs","+   mingw-w64 before the repo builds at all. brain needs none of this (pure Go, including","+   `modernc.org/sqlite`). Not a defect, but it belongs in the contributor docs.","+","+### What the run could not see","+","+The suite was exercised on an LF checkout, so **graph's test suite has never been run on a","+default CRLF Windows checkout**. brain's has (the kit cloned without touching","+`core.autocrlf` on a machine where it was on, and the suite was green), which is why brain","+can say the CRLF exposure is gofmt-only. Graph cannot say that yet. Given item 2 above, it","+is worth one run before anyone calls graph's Windows support complete."]}]}],"moreFiles":0,"changedFiles":["/Users/suhaan/devenv/GRAPH-FEEDBACK.md"]}},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"8cd7445b-701c-4b10-918b-31f4bb66aa90","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv","root":"/Users/suhaan/devenv","branch":"main","default_branch":null,"status":{"clean":false,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":1,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":274},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remote":"entirehq/devenv","visibility":"private"},"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[{"name":"origin","host":"github.com","remote":"entirehq/devenv","visibility":"private"}],"visibility_cache":[{"host":"github.com","remote":"entirehq/devenv","visibility":"private"},{"host":"github.com","remote":"entireio/entire-brain","visibility":"public"},{"host":"github.com","remote":"entireio/entire-graph","visibility":"public"},{"host":"github.com","remote":"entireio/cli","visibility":"public"}]}},"live_cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv","platform":"macos"}},"sandboxed":false,"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"45c646d9-55f2-4066-99d8-628fa2c42fb5","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"session_id":"1041b1a0-c015-41c2-a29f-4f2db2f6e0cb","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv","sessionId":"93ae8948-c612-4072-8e70-2750e80df9ce","version":"2.1.323","gitBranch":"main","slug":"floofy-discovering-bachman"}
{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"93ae8948-c612-4072-8e70-2750e80df9ce","hash":"53efd54bc431ba402d494a8146391e136595eb3d373067c8374d528e82626326","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01VMmHjYhq6JQ8ZWSTsrHHFx","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/suhaan/devenv && cat >> GRAPH-FEEDBACK.md <<'EOF'\n\n---\n\n## Reviewed 2026-11-18: four Windows draft PRs (#416–#419) — handoff to the graph lane\n\nWritten by the brain-lane session. **These four PRs are in your lane and I have taken no\naction on them: no approval, no merge, no comment, no rebase.** They were opened by a\nverification agent run on real Windows hardware (Windows 11 Pro 10.0.26200 amd64,\n`go1.27.4`, gcc 16.2.0 MinGW-w64 UCRT, non-administrator, Developer Mode off), on a\nmachine whose user profile is itself a git repository — so `%TEMP%` sits inside one, a\nconfiguration no CI runner has and one that is common for anyone keeping dotfiles in git.\n\nAll four are green on every leg: ubuntu, macos (12 shards) and windows-latest.\n\n| PR | Kind | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|\n| #416 `agentsetup` honours `GIT_CEILING_DIRECTORIES` | product + test | sound, one design question for you |\n| #417 skip symlink fixtures without the privilege | test only | sound |\n| #418 skip the verify replay when `sh` is absent | test only | sound, trivial |\n| #419 accept a leading UTF-8 BOM in a compact snapshot | product | correct, but **does not close the flow it describes** |\n\n### What I verified independently rather than taking from the PR bodies\n\n#416 claims its production blast radius is one command. That is true. Asked through your\nown tool rather than by grep:\n\n    entire graph neighbors --repo . --head --symbol OuterRepo --direction in\n    entire graph neighbors --repo . --head --symbol Context --file internal/agentsetup/migration.go --line 94 --direction in\n\n`Context` and `OuterRepo` have exactly one production caller between them,\n`graphAgentCommand` (`internal/cli/agents.go:36` and `:59`), and the `OuterRepo` site is\nalready guarded by `!isHomeDir(outer)`. So the user-visible effect really is confined to\nthe guide's destination and one warning.\n\nThe ceiling logic itself matches git's rule — stop before stepping *up into* a ceiling,\nstarting directory still examined, relative and empty entries ignored — and\n`TestContextHonoursGitCeilingDirectories` checks all seven cases against\n`git rev-parse --show-toplevel` with the same environment and cwd. Differential testing\nagainst git itself is the strongest form this check could take. Staying lexical rather\nthan resolving ceiling entries fails in the safe direction: a mismatch leaves the walk as\nunfenced as before, never more fenced.\n\n### The one I would not merge as a closing fix: #419\n\nWindows PowerShell 5.1's `>` defaults to **UTF-16LE**, not UTF-8-with-BOM. The PR's own\ntable shows a UTF-8 BOM only because that machine's profile had changed the default, and\nits \"NOT verified\" section says as much. So on a stock Windows box the documented flow\n\n    entire graph snapshot --repo . --format compact-ndjson > graph.compact.ndjson\n    entire graph snapshot-query --input graph.compact.ndjson --symbol Foo\n\n**still fails**, with a different error. The BOM skip is correct and worth having on its\nown terms — it matches what the git-config guard and `agentsetup`'s Claude-settings reader\nalready do — but the change that actually closes the flow is the `--output <file>` flag the\nPR proposes and leaves to you. Worth taking the fix; not worth closing the issue on it.\n\n### Decisions that are yours, not the agent's\n\n1. **Shared ceiling resolver, or two copies?** #416 adds `internal/agentsetup/gitceiling.go`\n   rather than importing `sem`'s resolver, because `agentsetup` today imports no internal\n   package. brain landed the same logic inline in `internal/agentsetup/migration.go` in its\n   own PR #654. That is now **three** copies of git's ceiling rule across two repos, already\n   diverging in shape. You and brain already share the guide renderer (graph #366 / brain\n   #447), so there is precedent for sharing this too. The PR explicitly invites you to say so.\n2. **graph has no `.gitattributes` at all.** With `core.autocrlf=true` (the Git for Windows\n   default) a stock Windows clone checks out every `.go` file with CRLF, and `gofmt -l -s .`\n   then lists all of them — so graph's gofmt gate cannot pass on a default Windows clone.\n   Every golden fixture is unpinned for the same reason. brain hit this too; the brain-lane\n   fix is `*.go text eol=lf` plus a class guard (branch `win/go-eol-lf`), verified with\n   `git add --renormalize .` rewriting zero files. The same two lines would do it here, and\n   it is cheap. Not my lane, so I have not written it for you.\n3. **`sem` symlink coverage.** #417 fixes the one `sem` site that failed and adds an\n   AST-based class guard, but the guard covers `agentsetup` only; `sem` has 156 `os.Symlink`\n   call sites across 48 test files with no shared helper to anchor a guard on. Whether that\n   is worth a helper is your call.\n4. **cgo stays a Windows prerequisite.** The tree-sitter grammars all `import \"C\"` with no\n   pure-Go fallback, so `GOOS=windows CGO_ENABLED=0 go build ./...` fails for every grammar.\n   Irrelevant for released binaries; a real barrier for a Windows contributor, who needs\n   mingw-w64 before the repo builds at all. brain needs none of this (pure Go, including\n   `modernc.org/sqlite`). Not a defect, but it belongs in the contributor docs.\n\n### What the run could not see\n\nThe suite was exercised on an LF checkout, so **graph's test suite has never been run on a\ndefault CRLF Windows checkout**. brain's has (the kit cloned without touching\n`core.autocrlf` on a machine where it was on, and the suite was green), which is why brain\ncan say the CRLF exposure is gofmt-only. Graph cannot say that yet. Given item 2 above, it\nis worth one run before anyone calls graph's Windows support complete.\nEOF\necho \"=== appended; new length: $(wc -l < GRAPH-FEEDBACK.md) lines ===\"; tail -6 GRAPH-FEEDBACK.md","description":"Append graph review handoff to feedback file"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}]}}
